\par\par \b \uc2 COMEX gold limps lower early, seen hemmed in range.  \b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2004\par 16:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 7 (Reuters) - U.S. gold futures retreated on Tuesday morning as last week's precious metals rally ran out of steam amid a lack of interest, despite fresh lows in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Gold has been consolidating since it hit a 16-year high of $458.70 in February futures on Thursday, in a bull move beefed up by almost daily new highs in the euro against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Volume has dried up. There is not data or news driving stuff," said a broker/analyst at a futures commission merchant.  \par \par \uc2 "While we did get a new high in the euro, it seems to be kind of a ho-hum thing and it's now off the high, and gold never really followed.  \par \par \uc2 "Gold seems to be following the euro on a directional basis, but not always to the same degree," he added.  \par \par \uc2 February gold futures on the New York Mercantile Exchange's COMEX division fell $2.40 at $453.50 an ounce by 10:32 a.m. EST (1532 GMT), trading from $456.90 to $453.30.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the market should keep to a range from about $450 to $455 an ounce, buffeted by flight-to-safety buying on the one hand and profit-taking at current strong prices on the other.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar reached a record low against the euro at $1.3470 earlier Tuesday, after a warning by European officials on the euro's rise fell on deaf ears, with investors determined to dump the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The euro later traded at $1.3424. The dollar also reached a 12-year low versus sterling beyond $1.95 .  \par \par \uc2 A soft greenback typically supports gold, which is denominated in dollars in global markets, as it becomes a better buy for non-U.S. investors.  \par \par \uc2 However, markets were growing cautious about a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from either Europe or \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the greenback's steady decline, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 ScotiaMocatta's December Money Matters report said good physical and investment demand for gold, including interest generated by new exchange-traded funds, could drive prices "considerably" higher over the long-term, if underlying imbalances in financial markets, such as a tumbling dollar, remain unchecked.  \par \par \uc2 But, in the run up to year-end, profit-taking might hit gold as book-squaring was likely, while any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the dollar's decline could act to derail gold's rally, ScotiaMocatta said.  \par \par \uc2 "However, even though a sell-off in gold may be quite sharp, it is likely to provide a good longer term buying opportunity," it added.  \par \par \uc2 Brokers viewed resistance in COMEX gold at $457.80 and $460 to $461, with support at $448.20-$449.50, and $432 and $420.  \par \par \uc2 Spot gold slid to $451.60/2.40, off from Monday's late quote in New York at $453.50/4.25 and well below last week's 16-year peak at $456.75. The late London fix on Tuesday was at $451.80.  \par \par \uc2 March silver ceded more ground following Monday's burst of long liquidation, slipping 7.3 cents to $7.89 an ounce, within a $7.98-$7.87 range. Last week, it scaled an eight-month peak at $8.235.  \par \par \uc2 Chartists pegged support at $7.84 and $7.55/57, with resistance at $8.235 and the spot high of $8.50 from April.  \par \par \uc2 Spot silver hit $7.82/85, off from $7.89/92 previously. The London fix was at $7.8425.  \par \par \uc2 January platinum fell $8.70 to $869 an ounce. Spot platinum touched $866.00/870.00.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par March palladium  dipped $1.05 to stand at $210.75. Spot        hit\par $205.50/209.50.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument 