\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Dec. 6  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,180 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2004\par 10:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA WATCH: The seven-year-old economic policy that earned the now retired Zhu Rongji the title Premier Deficit has been officially jettisoned as China's ruling Communists embark on a new strategy to sustain growth. (=CHINA WATCH column will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Asia will remain the world's most vibrant economic region in 2005, but growth will slow as the region faces headwinds from slowdowns in the U.S. and China and settles into a more mature expansion phase. ("=OUTLOOK 05: Asia's Growth Cycle Matures As Risks Grow," ran at 0358 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =TAIWAN VOTE: Standard & Poor's Ratings Services' downgrade of Taiwan's outlook ahead of Saturday's legislative elections highlights concerns a majority for the ruling party would worsen relations with China. ("=TAIWAN VOTE: Poll Result Could Raise Tensions With China," ran at 0817 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen may appear self-serving as it would leave the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bear the brunt of the greenback's fall. Not so, say Japan officials, who claim that Europe and China - not Japan - have been the main contributors to growth in the U.S. trade deficit. (=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia column will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's service-sector output extended its slump to a record four months in October, while household debt reached an all-time high in the third quarter, indicating that consumer demand is unlikely to pick up next year. ("=UPDATE: Korea Svc Sector In 4-Mo Slump, Debt Record High," ran at 0758 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China will continue to promote reforms of the country's "big four" state-owned banks as its banking system further opens to overseas firms, said China Banking Regulatory Commission Chairman Liu Mingkang. ("China Bank Regulator: Reform of State Banks Will Continue," ran at 0136 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's banking and seucurities regulators have issued new rules to lower the requirements for banks acting as custodians for securities investment funds. ("China Regulators Lower Requirements For Fund Custodians," ran at 0620 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to alter its neutral monetary policy stance, and almost certainly won't hike interest rates next week, despite some stronger-than-expected economic data and increased inflationary pressures. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: RBNZ Won't Change Neutral Stance," ran at 2022 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia's Asia Pulp & Paper expected to announce progress in $6 billion debt restructuring around 0900 GMT. (story will likely run around/after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's October preliminary trade surplus rose 18.2% on year to 7.89 billion ringgit ($2.08 billion) due to lower-than-expected growth for both exports and imports, the Ministry Of International Trade and Industry said Monday. ("=DATA SNAP: Malaysia Oct Trade Surplus MYR7.98B," first take ran at 0401 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's consumer price index grew for an 11th month in a row in November, propelled by higher oil, fruit and meat prices, the island's top statistics office said Monday. ("DATA SNAP: Taiwan CPI Up For 11th Straight Month In Nov," first take ran at 0820 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: The dollar bounced erratically late last week near five-year lows against the yen, but the options market failed to price in the turbulence. With little on the horizon to rescue the increasingly fragile dollar, three-month dollar/yen options continue to look attractive on dips, either outright or against the one-year in anticipation of an inverting volatilities curve. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Option Views: USD/JPY Vol Attractive As Actuals Surge," ran at 0554 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The New Zealand dollar Monday joined the global system for real-time \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions, known as continuous linked settlement, or CLS, the Reserve Bank of New Zealand said. ("NZ Dollar Joins Continuous Linked Settlement System -RBNZ," ran at 0223 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The Hong Kong government is taking a cautious stance on a proposal to allow casino \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the city to boost the local economy and create more jobs. ("HK Govt Cautious On Proposal To Allow Casino \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," ran at 0630 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar edged up against the yen in Asia Monday as Japanese investors bought on dips after its tumble last week on weak U.S. jobs data, but sentiment toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained bearish overall. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Up After Friday Plunge; Still Bearish," ran at 0527 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian grocery and liquor wholesaler Metcash Trading launched a takeover bid for Foodland Associated on Monday to diversify its earnings stream in an increasingly competitive retail market dominated by Woolworths and Coles Myer. After growing speculation that Foodland, which had a market capitalization of A$2.26 billion before the bid was unveiled versus Metcash's A$1.88 billion, was going to make a play for Metcash, the Sydney-based company surprised investors with its own complex bid. ("=UPDATE: Australia's Metcash Makes Play For Foodland," ran at 0827 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Fujitsu said Monday it will tie up with U.S. networking giant Cisco Systems in the area of routers and switches. The Japanese company, which currently has revenue of about Y40 billion in routers and switches, said its hopes to increase its revenue to Y60 billion three years from now.("Fujitsu Aims For Annual Router Ops Rev Of Y60B In 3 Yrs," first take ran at 0343 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: In its latest bid to enter the market for mobile phone services, Softbank Corp. said Monday its Softbank BB Corp. unit has applied to the Japanese government for a business license. ("=UPDATE: Softbank Makes New Bid To Start Mobile Phone Svc," ran at 0455 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: The labor union of Taiwan's largest integrated phone company Chunghwa Telecom Sunday voted in favor of a strike against the company's privatization if needed. But the union's vice executive secretary Chang Li-fen said Monday the union will continue to negotiate with the company's management and the government before it decides to go ahead with a strike. ("Taiwan's Chunghwa Telecom Workers Vote In Favor Of Strike," ran at 0159 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Samsung Electronics dazzled consumers in 2004 with photo-snapping cellphones and super-slim television sets. While earnings growth may slow down in 2005 due to an oversupply of products in key segments, Samsung will continue outperforming its rivals next year thanks to hefty capital spending on R&D and efficient production technology. ("=OUTLOOK 05: Samsung To Stand Out From Pack In Tough Year," ran at 0855 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Uncertainties abound as fund managers prepare for a choppy ride for equities in 2005. Stock markets in Asia, though, may well outperform those in other parts of the world. ("=OUTLOOK 05:Fund Managers Expect Choppy Year For Stocks," ran at 0852 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: China Aviation Oil (Singapore) Corp.'s suspended chief executive Chen Jiulin will return "sometime this week," the company said Monday, confirming reports he will be returning to assist in investigations concerning CAO's US$550 million trading losses. ("China Aviation Oil CEO To Return To Singapore This Wk," first take ran at 0736 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: After years of stagnation, shares of Australian drinks company Foster's Group have perked up in the past few months as investors bet on a profit revival for its underperforming wine unit. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Investors Prepare For Foster's Return," ran at 2300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sanyo Electric said Monday it has begun trial semiconductor production at its quake-hit factory in Niigata prefecture, northern Japan. The Japanese consumer electronics maker shut down its largest chip-making factory in Ojiya since Oct. 23 when a 6.8-magnitude earthquake hit the Niigata area. ("Sanyo Elec Begins Trial Chip Output At Quake-Idled Plant," ran at 0747 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Mitsubishi Motors said Monday it may consider expanding the range of its ties with Nissan Motor after a report that the two automakers are negotiating a broad-based alliance in their passenger car \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nissan declined to comment. ("Mitsubishi Motors: Mulling Expanding Ties With Nissan," first take ran at 2351 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sales of imported cars, trucks and buses in Japan rose 1.1% in November from a year earlier, marking the first increase in two months, the Japan Automobile Importers Association said Monday. ("Japan November Imported Auto Sales Up 1.1% On Year," ran at 0200 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Indian drug makers go back to the future on New Year's Day 2005, when a proposed new patent law ends their 32-year-old practice of churning out copycat medicines for the local market. But this time, the readoption of product patents could unleash huge gains for local drug makers through outsourcing, clinical research, and stronger alliances with global pharmaceutical companies. (=OUTLOOK 05 story will run by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Ostensibly bowing to regulatory and commercial pressures as it prepares for full privatization, Australia's largest telephone company, Telstra Corp., on Monday unveiled a separate structure for its wholesale business and reorganized its senior executive team. ("=UPDATE: Telstra Tweaks Structure; Move Labeled Cosmetic," ran at 0524 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: European telecommunications firm Skylogic signs a deal with China Satcom. (INTERVIEW with Skylogic executive expected to run after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: A key court ruling and a major power-plant sale signal a possible regulatory easing that could revive interest in the Philippines' stock market. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Philippine Court Ruling Bolsters Stks," ran at 0249 GMT) [ 06-12-04 0902GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Determined to fend off a hostile takeover bid by its major shareholder, Australian dairy target National Foods Monday said its shares are worth up to 22% more than Fonterra Cooperative Group's A$1.3 billion offer. The board of National Foods valued itself between A$6.11 and A$6.65 a share on a standalone basis, compared with Fonterra's A$5.45-a-share bid and most analyst estimates of up to A$6.00 a share. ("=UPDATE: Australia's Natl Foods Ups Ante In Milk Fight," ran at 0630 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: National carrier Air New Zealand said Monday that its rights issue was fully subscribed, and the company's new shares will begin trading on the stock exchange Friday. ("Air New Zealand Rights Issue Well Supported By Investors," ran at 2346 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Australia's WMC Resources said Monday that it will spend up to A$650 million expanding its nickel unit later this decade as it tries to fight off Xstrata's A$7.4 billion hostile bid. ("=UPDATE: Australia's WMC Plans A$650M Nickel Expansion," ran at 0759 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: OneSteel's troubled Whyalla plant has hit another snag, further bruising investor confidence as the Australian steelmaker's return to production is delayed. A clean-up \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the South Australian facility has run into some hurdles, stalling a restart by up to a week. ("=UPDATE: Furnace Cleanout Delays OneSteel Plant Restart," ran at 0531 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: The planned 2005 launch in India of futures trading in crude oil and refined products will allow refiners to hedge their price risks, but local energy analysts say low trading volumes may limit the success of the venture. ("=OUTLOOK 05: India Prepares For Trading In Oil Futures," ran at 0417 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand electricity generation from hydro and wind sources in the third quarter to Sept. 30 rose 27% from the same quarter in 2003, according to official figures published Monday. ("NZ's 3Q Hydro, Wind Generation Rose 27% On Year" ran at 2257 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Australia is on track to meet a reduction in greenhouse gas emissions as set out in the Kyoto Protocol, which the government hasn't ratified. ("Australia On Track To Meet Kyoto Greenhouse Target," ran at 0000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Australian grain growers have described as "obscene" what they say are the high level of U.S. farm subsidies, much of which go to a small number of farm corporations. ("Australian Grain Growers Criticize US Farm Subsidies," ran at 0117 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: State-owned Vietnam Steel Corp., or Vinasteel, the country's largest steel producer, is seeking government approval for its plan to build a $3 billion steel complex in Central Vietnam,a company official said Monday. ("Vietnam Mulls Building $3B Steel Complex, Seeks Govt OK," ran at 0536 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: The higher global oil prices in recent months have led to a revival in interest on bio-fules, but Thailand's sugar cane growers may have to wait well beyond 2005 to see the commercial use of sugar cane as a feedstock in ethanol production. ("OUTLOOK 05:Asia's Cane-Based Ethanol Plans May Take Time," ran at 0359 GMT) [ 06-12-04 0902GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041206e0c6000dn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
