\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Midday  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Stable At Slightly Higher Levels  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings  \par \uc2 529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 06, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \par \uc2 The dollar is stable at slightly higher levels midday in Europe Monday after reports of an attack on the U.S. consulate in Jeddah helped bring an end to early gains. A combination of weekend talk of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and signs that global investors are starting to wind down activity before the year end helped to underpin the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the start of the session. The dollar improved despite last Friday's disappointingly small 112,000 rise in non-farm payrolls in November. It shows that economic fundamentals aren't having the same impact they once had. At least some of the shift in sentiment was due to recent media reports about the possibility of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the dollar from falling much lower. While one report indicated that the U.S. won't consider action until the euro rises as far as $1.4500, another suggested that Japanese and European officials have discussed joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has warned the U.S. about the risk of an enormous capital flight from the dollar. However, another report, from the New York Times Monday, quoted Masatsuga Asakawa, the senior \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official in charge of managing the U.S. Treasury portfolio, as saying there are no plans to change the current policy of buying U.S. Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 "Imagine that tomorrow people hear, 'Hey Japan has decided to divert from the dollar to the euro'," Asakawa was quoted as saying. "That would create a hugely undesirable impact on U.S. Treasurys and we have no intention at all to make an unfortunate impact on the U.S. Treasury market." All the same, with German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder visiting Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi in Tokyo later this week, the topic of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain at the forefront of people's minds. By midmorning, however, reports that the U.S. consulate in Jeddah was under attack were enough to bring an abrupt end to any dollar buying, and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drifted back slightly as crude oil prices ticked higher. Although the attack was soon over, with three Saudi security guards killed, the price of crude futures on the New York Mercantile Exchange rose to $42.88 a barrel, another 11 cents from $42.77/bbl late in the Asian day, which was already up 23 cents from the U.S. close Friday. By 1200 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y102.29, still well up from Y102.04 late Friday in New York but down from an earlier high at Y102.51. After sinking as far as $1.3419, the euro has rebounded to $1.3436 but is still down from $1.3455 late Friday in New York and a record high of $1.3461 reached earlier that day. The pound is down at $1.9410 from $1.9437, while the dollar is up at CHF1.1368 from CHF1.1302.  \par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041206e0c60040m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
