\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Up After Friday Plunge; Still Bearish  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara  \par \uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 06, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \par \uc2 The dollar edged up against the yen in Asia Monday as Japanese investors bought on dips after its tumble last week on weak U.S. jobs data, but sentiment toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained bearish overall. Against the euro, the dollar also gained slightly because traders were reluctant to push up the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its rise to a new all-time high of $1.3461 in New York trading Friday. But despite the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slight recovery, many market players expect it to head even lower against the yen and euro this week, amid widespread speculation Washington won't mind a gradual dollar weakening to help narrow the U.S.'s ballooning current account deficit. Some players expect the dollar to head down for a test of the November 26, 1999 low of Y101.25 this week, and they predict that the euro will strengthen further to test strong psychological resistance at $1.3500. "It's unlikely that any economic indicators or monetary-policy-related events scheduled worldwide this week will have any effect on the dollar's broad downtrend," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase Bank of Tokyo. One of the few factors supporting the dollar is speculation that Japanese and European monetary authorities might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent their respective home \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating further against the U.S. unit. Kaoru Yosano, chairman of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party's policy council, told the U.K. Observer newspaper that Japan will ask the United States "for a strong dollar policy" and warned Washington that further falls in the greenback could trigger an "enormous capital flight from the dollar." But the reported remarks didn't help the dollar because Yosano isn't responsible for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which is the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's jurisdiction. In any case, market players believe the U.S., while on the surface calling for a strong dollar, will continue to tolerate its weakening, traders said. "Japan and Europe will take joint action only if the U.S. gives a nod to such a move," which is highly unlikely at this point, said Akifumi Uchida, senior manager of financial products and marketing at Sumitomo Trust & Banking. "But it's a different story if speculative moves clearly begin to dominate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he said, adding that "it won't be surprising" if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  upon a dollar fall below Y100.00 while the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might step into the market if the euro rises above $1.3500.  \par \par \uc2 Interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT  \par \par \uc2 \f2                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg\par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High\par    Low      12/31\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/JPY Yen              102.14-16  102.13-21  +0.01  102.28  101.93   -4.84\par EUR/USD Euro             1.3446-47  1.3441-45  +0.04  1.3453  1.3429   +6.75\par GBP/USD Sterling         1.9411-16  1.9420-25  -0.05  1.9444  1.9396   +8.67\par USD/