\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar firms but bearish sentiment lingers.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrea Ricci  \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2004\par 18:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 6 (Reuters) - The dollar rose on Monday in a retracement from last week's steep losses, but dealers said the bias toward a weaker greenback remained intact.  \par \par \uc2 With little on the U.S. economic calendar this week, dealers said the market was focused on the factors that have spurred heavy dollar sales in recent weeks, namely the gaping U.S. current account deficit and the related issue of central banks' diversification of reserves away from the dollar, often to the benefit of the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "They want to diversify and will continue to do so into the near future, so any rally in the dollar will be met by sellers coming out of the woodwork," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at ING Capital Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 A Bank for International Settlements quarterly report released on Sunday showed that OPEC countries have cut their share of deposits in dollars by over 13 percentage points in the last three years, mainly to the euro's advantage.  \par \par \uc2 In early afternoon trading in New York, the euro was down 0.17 percent at $1.3431 , below record highs near $1.3460 touched on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also rose 0.58 percent to 102.64 yen , nearly a full yen above the near five-year lows hit last week.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the threat of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was lending the dollar some support.  \par \par \uc2 European finance ministers meeting later on Monday in Brussels were expected to voice concerns about the euro's rise, but most dealers see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as unlikely at this point.  \par \par \uc2 Both the euro and the dollar rose against the Swiss franc after Swiss National Bank President Jean-Pierre Roth said weaker economic growth and high oil prices would prompt central banks to shy away from raising interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar gained 0.65 percent to 1.1379 Swiss francs  while the euro\par rose 0.50 percent to 1.5286 francs          .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling slipped 0.1 percent to $1.9418 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar soared against the Canadian dollar, jumping nearly 1 percent to C$1.2015 by midday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Steven Englander, chief North American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for Barclays Capital in New York, said the Canadian dollar, which has recently traded at 12-year highs against the U.S. dollar, was starting to lose some of its allure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The two things supporting dollar/Canada has been a run of good Canadian economic data and officials being very tolerant of the Canadian dollar's appreciation," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But officials have started to change their tune and the latest payrolls number was not that strong," Englander added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SNOW OUT?  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers said the market was also preoccupied with speculation over who would replace U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow after The New York Times said President George W. Bush had decided to replace him, possibly with White House Chief of Staff Andrew Card.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The dollar has not rallied on the back of this discussion and to me that says the market's interpretation is that Card's appointment would be more of the same," said Lara Rhame, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury has long maintained that it has a "strong dollar" policy, but many dealers believe the administration has no plans to stand in the way of the dollar's decline as long as it remains orderly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital's Englander said how the U.S. interprets its dollar policy would depend on how the economy fares, regardless of who has the top job at Treasury.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "One of the things that people have to remember is that a lot depends on what U.S. interests are," said Englander.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Snow and his predecessor, Paul O'Neill, growth was the issue, so a more competitive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was viewed as positive for the economy, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But "if inflation picks up later this year, it would be in the interest of the United States not to let the dollar drop," Englander said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041206e0c600a44\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
