\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rises but outlook remains bearish.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrea Ricci  \par \uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2004\par 16:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comments; changes dateline, previous LONDON, changes byline)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 6 (Reuters) - The dollar rose on Monday in a retracement from last week's steep losses but dealers said the bias for a weaker dollar remained intact.  \par \par \uc2 With little on the U.S. economic calendar this week, dealers said the market was focused on the factors that have spurred heavy dollar sales in recent weeks, namely the gaping U.S. current account gap and the related issue of central banks' diversification of reserves away from the dollar, often to the benefit of the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "They want to diversify and will continue to do so into the near future, so any rally in the dollar will be met by sellers coming out of the woodwork," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at ING Capital Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said only the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was lending the dollar support.  \par \par \uc2 At midmorning in New York, the euro was down 0.13 percent at $1.3437, not far from record highs near $1.3460 achieved on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 0.39 percent to 102.46 yen, more than a half yen over five-year lows hit last week.  \par \par \uc2 Both the euro and the dollar rose against the Swiss franc after Swiss National Bank President Jean-Pierre Roth said weaker economic growth and high oil prices would prompt central banks to shy away from raising interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar gained a half percent to 1.1365 Swiss francs while the euro rose 0.37 percent to 1.5278 francs.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling slipped 0.1 percent to $1.9418.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the market was preoccupied on Monday with speculation over who would replace U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow after the New York Times said President George W. Bush had decided to replace Snow, possibly with White House Chief of Staff Andrew Card.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar has not rallied on the back of this discussion and to me that says the market's interpretation is that Card's appointment would be more of the same," said Lara Rhame, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury has long maintained that it has a strong dollar policy. But many dealers believe the administration has no plans to stand in the way of the dollar's decline as long as it remains orderly.  \par \par \uc2 RESERVE SHIFTS  \par \par \uc2 A Bank for International Settlements report released on Sunday provided evidence for the shifting of official reserves out of dollars.  \par \par \uc2 In its quarterly report, the BIS said OPEC countries have cut the share of their deposits in dollars by over 13 percentage points in the last three years, mainly to the euro's advantage.  \par \par \uc2 "The reserve diversification story is a long term negative factor for the dollar," said Adam Myers, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Societe Generale in London. "It is feeding into worries over how the U.S. will finance its deficit and the cyclical story is being forgotten."  \par \par \uc2 In Japan, however, a senior finance ministry official reiterated the ministry's stance that it has no plan to divert its massive foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves out of dollars and into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 A British newspaper on Sunday suggested that Japan was threatening a huge dollar sell-off, but Masatsugu Asakawa, head of the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets division, said on Monday Japan had no plan to divert from dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Brazil, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The real is trading at its strongest level in two-and-a-half years, helped by strong economic data and heavy inflows from exports and overseas corporate bond sales.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Christina Fincher in London and Terry Wade in Sao Paulo).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041206e0c60094h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
