\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar within sight of record low versus euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2004\par 13:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 6 (Reuters) - The dollar re-tested its record low against the euro and teetered above five-year lows versus the yen on Monday as traders wondered how long European and Japanese policymakers would tolerate the greenback's slide.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Chief Cabinet Secretary Hiroyuki Hosada said the dollar's current rate against the yen did not reflect economic fundamentals, a sentiment echoed by vice finance minister Koichi Hosokawa who reiterated Japan would take "bold action" if needed.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone finance ministers meet in Brussels later on Monday and are also expected to discuss the impact of the dollar's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a question that is looming large but at the moment the market does not view it as imminent," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 Sentiment towards the dollar deteriorated further after data on Friday showed the U.S. economy generated fewer jobs in November than expected.  \par \par \uc2 "The payrolls figures suggest the cyclical reasons for buying the dollar are not enough to outweigh the structural reasons for selling," said Jalinoos.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar stood at 102.34 yen  at 1108 GMT, having fallen on Friday to\par around 101.83 yen, its lowest since January 2000. The greenback fetched\par $1.3450 against the euro       , having fallen to around $1.3460 on\par Friday in the wake of the disappointing payrolls report.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STRUCTURAL CONCERNS  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar has suffered incessant selling in the past two months on renewed worries about the giant U.S. trade and budget deficits and a widespread belief a weaker dollar is needed - and perhaps wanted by Washington - to correct them.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data at the weekend suggesting oil-producing countries have already started diversifying reserves out of dollars only highlighted the negative long-term backdrop for the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In its quarterly report released on Sunday, the Bank for International Settlements said OPEC countries have cut the share of their deposits in dollars by over 13 percentage points in the last three years, mainly to the euro's advantage.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The reserve diversification story is a long term negative factor for the dollar," said Adam Myers, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Societe Generale. "It is feeding into worries over how the U.S. will finance its deficit and the cyclical story is being forgotten."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, the prospect of a higher U.S. Fed funds rate - which makes dollar deposits more attractive - is providing little support to the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Reuters poll last week found all 20 primary dealers surveyed expected the Fed to raise its key rate by a quarter percentage point to 2.25 percent at its December meeting. Sixteen of the 20 looked for another quarter-point tightening in February.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANKS EYED  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese officials in particular have stepped up their warnings in recent days about taking dollar-buying action, calling recent market moves volatile and hinting at possible coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But traders say the market will keep driving the dollar lower until \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  occurs. Some believe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may ultimately act alone if yen strength accelerates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since early October the yen has jumped nearly 9 percent against the dollar, while the euro has surged more than 9 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking on Monday, ECB Governing Council member Axel Weber said short-term moves in exchange rates could cause a shift in competitive relationships and were "highly unwelcome". But he did little to convince the market that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb the euro's strength against the dollar was imminent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We have had no concrete signals that the ECB is going to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Myers. "People are looking for stronger verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  first."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swiss franc fell against both the euro and dollar after Swiss National Bank President Jean-Pierre Roth said weaker economic growth and high oil prices would prompt central banks to shy away from raising interest rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market took the comments as an indication Switzerland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would not raise rates at its next quarterly policy meeting on December 16.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041206e0c600796\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
