\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar teeters above lows,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  threat eyed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 490 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2004\par 10:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 6 (Reuters) - The dollar teetered above last week's record low against the euro and five-year low versus the yen on Monday as traders wondered how long European and Japanese policymakers would tolerate the greenback's slide.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's Chief Cabinet Secretary Hiroyuki Hosada said the dollar's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate against the yen did not reflect economic fundamentals, a sentiment echoed by vice finance minister Koichi Hosokawa who reiterated Japan would take "bold action" if needed.   \par \par \uc2  Euro zone finance ministers meet in Brussels later on Monday and are also expected to discuss the impact of the dollar's weakness.   \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a question that is looming large but at the moment the market does not view it as imminent," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers said sentiment towards the dollar remained extremely bearish, particularly after data on Friday showed the U.S. economy generated fewer jobs in November than expected.   \par \par \uc2  "The payrolls figures suggest the cyclical reasons for buying the dollar are not enough to outweigh the structural reasons for selling," said Jalinoos.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 102.45 yen at 0855 GMT, having fallen on Friday to around 101.83 yen, its lowest since January 2000. The greenback fetched $1.3438 against the euro, having fallen to around $1.3460 on Friday in the wake of the disappointing payrolls report.   \par \par \uc2  RATES NOT A FACTOR   \par \par \uc2  The dollar has suffered incessant selling in the past two months on renewed worries about the giant U.S. trade and budget deficits and a widespread belief a weaker dollar is needed - and perhaps wanted by Washington - to correct them.   \par \par \uc2  The prospect of a higher U.S. Fed funds rate - which makes dollar deposits more attractive - has mattered little to markets.   \par \par \uc2  Despite the poor jobs data, a Reuters poll found that all 20 primary dealers surveyed expected the Fed to raise its key rate by a quarter percentage point to 2.25 percent at its December meeting. Sixteen of the 20 looked for another quarter-point tightening in February.   \par \par \uc2  "The interest rate outlook isn't a factor for the market," said Kikuko Takeda, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2  "I think we should be prepared to see another round of dollar selling," she said, adding the market would likely target a fall towards 100 yen and a rise in the euro to $1.35.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials in particular have stepped up their warnings in recent days about taking dollar-buying action, calling recent market moves volatile and hinting at possible coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  But traders say the market will keep driving the dollar lower until \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurs. Some believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may ultimately act alone if yen strength accelerates.   \par \par \uc2  Since early October the yen has jumped nearly 9 percent against the dollar, while the euro has surged more than 9 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041206e0c6006bz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
