\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Dec 3  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 10:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Atsushi Mizuno, who the government approved Friday as a Bank of Japan policy board member to replace Teizo Taya, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely soon achieve its goal of avoiding a deflationary spiral and stabilizing financial markets. (UPDATE to run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese firms boosted their capital investment in the third quarter, a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey showed Friday, spurring expectations for an upward revision to previously released weak economic growth data. ("=UPDATE: Japan Corp Survey Spurs Hopes For 3Q GDP Hike," ran at 0622 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: Philippine exports gathered more steam in October, raising the government's optimism that the economy's major driver will grow 10% for the whole year. ("=UPDATE: Philippine Exports Gather Steam In Oct," ran at 0424 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: The International Finance Corp. priced its inaugural Islamic three-year ringgit bond at par to yield 2.88%, 10 basis points below the comparable three-year Malaysian Government Securities, one of the lead managers said Friday. ("IFC Islamic Malaysia MYR500M Bond Price 10 BPs Below MGS," first take ran at 0726 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: The end of 30 years of global garment quotas should be a huge long-term boon for China's low-cost producers. But in the short run, it may instead mean a trade war between Washington and Beijing. ("=OUTLOOK 05: Fiber Pact's End May Mean China-US Trade War," the first in a feature series about the regional impact of the Dec. 31 expiry of the Multifiber Agreement, ran at 0552 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =Australia Corp Bonds: Activity in Australia's corporate bond market this week showed few signs of easing up ahead of year-end, with the spotlight falling on local players Telstra Corp. and National Australia Bank Ltd. ("=Australia Corp Bonds: No Let Up On Supply Side," ran at 0456 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Market chatter that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Europe might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the dollar nudged it up this week from multiyear lows. But such coordinated action looks unlikely. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia:Concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  By \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Europe Unlikely," ran at 0411 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The head of Japan's government Tax Commission said Friday the nation will need to lift its current 5% consumption tax to around 15% in the future to fund growing social security costs. ("Japan Govt Tax Panel Head:Consumption Tax Could Reach 15%," ran at 0815 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's banking system appeared awash in cash over the past week, putting the treasury bond market in a comfortable position to respond to any decisions made at a weekend meeting of policy makers. ("=China Money Mkts Week: Banking System Awash In Liquidity," first take ran at 0413 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =INSIDE INDOCHINA: Myanmar's prime minister, Soe Win, is a happy man. Just home from a summit at which he had expected to receive a tongue lashing, and possibly the threat of action if his government didn't speed up political reform, he can instead crow about his nation's much-needed entry into the lucrative global marketplace and his military junta's secure grip on power. ("=INSIDE INDOCHINA:Myanmar Gains Strength As Asean Dithers," ran at 0840 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's economy is expected to expand 5.7% on year in the current quarter to bring full-year growth to 8.3%, according to a quarterly \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey of economists published Friday. ("Singapore MAS: Economists' Avg 4Q GDP Forecast +5.7%," ran at 0400 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Inflation in India remained unchanged at 7.34% on year in the week to Nov. 20, due largely to lower fuel prices and a statistical base effect, data issued Friday by the Commerce Ministry showed. ("India WPI +7.34% On Yr In Week Ended Nov 20; +7.34% Pvs," ran at 0640 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The dollar may hold around Y103.00 against the yen this month but will resume its downtrend next year due to the expected persistence of a bloated U.S. trade deficit and U.S. nonchalance about the weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mizuho Research Institute's chief economist says.("=INTERVIEW: Dollar May Hold At Y103, But Only Briefly," ran at 0531 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China Commerce Minister Bo Xilai to speak at the American Chamber of Commerce annual government appreciation dinner around 1100 GMT. (Story possible late Friday)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's Commercial Affairs Department, or CAD, which probes white-collar crime, has started investigating China Aviation Oil Singapore Corp., the Singapore Exchange said Friday. ("Singapore's CAD Investigates China Aviation Oil - SGX," ran at 0825 GMT; UPDATE to run around 1045 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Suntec Real Estate Investment Trust Friday priced its initial public offering at S$1.00 a unit, making the S$722 million offering the biggest in Singapore this year, a source familiar with the deal said. ("Singapore Suntec REIT IPO Priced At S$1.00/Unit - Source," first take ran at 0315 GMT; story likely to be updated after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Conceding that irreconcilable differences with Telstra Corp.'s board forced his resignation, chief executive Ziggy Switkowski hinted Friday that one of his parting acts will be a shake-up of the corporation's structure to smooth the way for full privatization. ("=UPDATE: Spurned Telstra CEO To Steer Revamp As Last Act," ran at 0319 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China's second largest telecom equipment maker ZTE Corp. Friday priced its initial public offering at HK$22 a share, the top end of an indicative range, to raise the equivalent of US$398 million, according to sponsor Goldman Sachs. ("China ZTE Prices HK IPO At Top End, HK$22.0/Shr - Goldman," first take ran at 0357 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Suzuki Motor Corp. will suspend planned extra production at its domestic plants for a total of three days this month due to a shortage of steel sheet, a Suzuki spokesman said Friday. ("Suzuki Motor To Suspend Extra Output On Steel Shortage," ran at 0158 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Kumho Tire Co., the world's ninth biggest tire maker, is considering a dual listing in Seoul and New York or London by the first half of next year, a source familiar with the matter said Friday. ("S Korea Kumho Tire Mulls Dual Listing By 1H '05 - Source," ran at 0248 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: The ability to charge more for loans might spark a rally in the bank shares of most countries, but stocks in China's handful of listed banks have barely stirred since authorities loosened the rigid interest-rate regime at the end of October. ("=ASIAN STOCK FOCUS:No Upside In Sight For China Bank Shrs," ran at 0002 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Johnson Electric expected to report first-half results. (Story to run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Directories business Yellow Pages (Singapore) Ltd. priced its shares at S$1.66 a piece, raising S$185 million in Singapore's third biggest initial public offering this year, a source familiar with the transaction said Friday. ("Yellow Pages Singapore Prices IPO At S$1.66/Shr - Source," first take ran at 0818 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: As with many local investment decisions these days, China will play a key role next year in deciding just how far north of 4,000 points the Australian share market will travel. ("=China Factor Remains For Australian Share Market In 2005," ran at 0828 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: The robust Indian stock market may be due for a breather, as investors wait for earnings to catch up with soaring share prices, some fund managers and analysts say. ("=ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: India's Stk Mkt May Pause For Breath," ran at 0244 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Ito-Yokado Co. said Friday its newly launched joint venture in China will open two fresh-food supermarket stores in Beijing in the spring of 2005. ("Ito-Yokado JV Plans Beijing Fresh-Food Stores Next Spring," ran at 0737 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: In a boost for its multibillion dollar Gulf of Mexico play, Anglo-Australian resources group BHP Billiton Friday lifted its production outlook for the Atlantis oil development by one-third. But amid rising world steel and labor costs, the cost of Atlantis has also risen by around 11%, BHP energy president Phil Aiken told analysts at a Sydney briefing. ("=UPDATE:Gulf Of Mexico To Become Core Energy Unit For BHP," ran at 0628 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: The Indian unit of Royal Dutch/Shell Group has resumed gasoline and diesel retail \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country after a gap of nearly three decades. ("Shell Resumes Gasoline, Diesel Retail Ops In India - Exec," ran at 0735 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese engineering firm JGC Corp. (1963.TO) said Friday it has received an order for basic designing of two core oil refinery units for a planned integrated petroleum complex in Saudi Arabia. ("Japan's JGC Gets Design Order For Saudi Refinery Units," ran at 0244 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA CRUDE OUTLOOK: Heavier crude supplies, both sour and sweet, will continue firming as Asian buying interest shifts away from light grades after months of strong demand. (ASIA CRUDE OUTLOOK to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA OIL PRODUCTS OUTLOOK: Supply and demand fundamentals will start to play a bigger part in determining Asian oil products prices next week. ("=ASIA OIL PRODUCTS OUTLOOK: Fundamentals Play Bigger Role," ran at 0832 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Australia's Trade Minister Mark Vaile said he favors replicating easier foreign investment rules contained in the U.S. free trade agreement to some other nations. ("Australian Trade Minister Favors Easier Investment Rules," ran at 0551 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: State-owned China Minmetals Corp. remains interested in taking over Canada's Noranda Inc. (NRD) even though its exclusive-negotiation period expired in November, said Minmetals' spokesman at Burson-Marsteller Public Relations Co. Friday. ("China Minmetals Still Interested In Taking Over Noranda," ran at 0519 GMT) [ 03-12-04 0920GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Japanese government holds nearly all its strategic reserves in crude oil and is unlikely to start building state-owned stocks of primary refined oil products such as gasoline and kerosene, a Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry official said Friday. ("Japan Unlikely To Build Strategic Reserves In Gasoline," ran at 0606 GMT) [ 03-12-04 0920GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041203e0c3000d7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
