\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar wins reprieve ahead of U.S. jobs report.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 517 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 3 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied a cent above this week's record low against the euro on Friday as investors lightened bets on further dollar losses ahead of key U.S. jobs figures.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit lifetime lows against the euro and 5-year lows versus the yen in early trade on Thursday but later rebounded on nervousness that market positions had become extreme and central banks could take action to curb the greenback's slide.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers were also reluctant to pound the greenback ahead of the November U.S. jobs report at 1330 GMT. An upbeat reading could highlight the relative strength of the U.S. economy and the forgotten attraction of U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 "We're seeing some squaring up of positions ahead of the jobs data," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered, adding the release would be a key test of dollar sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 "If the dollar can't benefit from a strong number, it would give a very bearish signal."  \par \par \uc2 Concerns over the U.S. trade and budget deficits have weighed heavily on the dollar which has fallen for 10 consecutive weeks against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar stood at $1.3280 to the euro at 0900 GMT, having recovered a cent from record lows hit the previous session. Against the yen, the dollar was slightly higher at 103.36.  \par \par \uc2 DATA WATCH  \par \par \uc2 A strong U.S. jobs report would cement the case for further rate hikes by the U.S. Federal Reserve, bolstering the attraction of the dollar to foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 Economists' median forecast is for 180,000 new jobs to have been generated in November, below October's 337,000 gain but still enough to persuade the Federal Reserve to raise rates again later this month.  \par \par \uc2 "Recent U.S. data has been pretty good, so there may be an expectation that the payroll figures will shift the focus of the market to the relative strength of the U.S. economy," said Kikuko Takeda, market economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 Many in the market were sceptical, however, recalling the dollar failed to gain on surprisingly strong job figures for October, overwhelmed by concerns about the United States' ability to finance its gaping current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese and the euro zone policymakers have expressed concern that the dollar's rapid fall could damage their economies and some traders said the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also keeping the dollar off its lows.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki reiterated on Friday that the yen's recent rise had been rapid and Japan would take action if needed.  \par \par \uc2 Data out of the euro zone highlighted how the strong euro had affected firms' competitiveness with the Reuters Eurozone Business Activity Index falling to 52.6 in November, its lowest in 15 months.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said on Thursday that recent moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were unwelcome but gave little indication that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the euro against the dollar was on cards.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041204e0c3002sw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
