\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Yen still good for Japan exports, dollar slide aside.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs  \par \uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 08:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 3 (Reuters) - For all the yen's dramatic gains against the dollar of late, its surprising steadiness against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may offer clues on why \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been so trigger-happy on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 While the yen has risen 8 percent against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two months, it has dithered against the euro, British pound and Australian dollar and stands lower against all of them since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  began in January 2003.  \par \par \uc2 That means the yen is still helping Japanese export growth to countries that, taken together, are also crucial to economic growth and corporate profits.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, Japan's overall exports hit a record high in October,  \par \par \uc2 "The popular tendency is to look only at the dollar/yen rate, and that's becoming less valid over time," said Jeff Young, head of economic analysis at Nikko Citigroup in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "In both broad and real terms, the yen has been on the soft side of its long-term ranges for some time now and that's provided important support for the economy," he said.  \par \par \uc2 NOT SO STRONG A YEN  \par \par \uc2 The yen has shed nearly 17 percent against the Australian dollar since January 2003 - despite Japan's massive current account surplus compared with Australia's gaping deficit. In that same period, the yen is down about 9 percent against the euro and almost 4 percent against the pound.  \par \par \uc2 Taking into account the yen's performance against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of trading partners and their respective inflation rates, the value of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is little changed from March.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's index for the yen's real effective exchange rate stands at 120.6 through Nov. 17, compared with 120.1 in March and a recent peak of 124.2 in November 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo, said the yen's sluggishness against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was due in part to "overhang" from the large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between January 2003 and March this year.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a whopping 35 trillion yen ($339 billion) stemming its rise against the dollar and weakening it against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "A lot of people were left holding yen," Gittler said.  \par \par \uc2 The current threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is real, especially after Japanese officials stepped up their media campaign this week with hints that there might be coordinated action with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But analysts still believe the Japanese government is unlikely to embark on another dollar-buying binge to rein in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 That's because Japan is in a stronger shape than in January 2003, when the dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also used as a way to pump liquidity into the deflation-trapped economy.  \par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showing a surprisingly big 14.4 percent jump in third-quarter capital spending compared with the same period a year earlier underscored the economy's heartier condition.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the U.S. and Europe are demanding that China revalue its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and such a move would be expected to lift the yen with it. Trying to fight such an appreciation would be politically difficult for Japan, given the trade frictions already brewing over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 THE EXPORT FACTOR  \par \par \uc2 As for exports, while business leaders have complained about the yen, those complaints have not been nearly as loud this time around as in other episodes when the dollar has dropped down towards the 100 yen level.  \par \par \uc2 Exports remain on the rise, even if the growth rate is not quite as roaring as it was earlier in the year. Even though the United States and China are key export markets, they only account for about a third of Japanese external demand.  \par \par \uc2 On an unadjusted basis, exports expanded at an 11.8 percent year-on-year pace in October - only slightly below the average of the previous six months.  \par \par \uc2 While exports to the United States were up a tepid 3.4 percent compared to the previous year, exports to Asia including China were up 12.6 percent, exports to China alone jumped 20 percent and exports to the European Union increased 10.8 percent. ($1=103.34 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041203e0c3006bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
