\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar enjoys solace ahead of U.S. jobs report.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 08:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 3 (Reuters) - The dollar edged up on Friday, building on the previous day's rebound from multi-year lows on speculation that a strong reading for upcoming U.S. jobs data could highlight the long-forgotten attraction of U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 Speculators locked in profits from weeks of dollar-selling, which had driven the yen to a five-year peak and the euro to record highs on Thursday, wary of the possibility, albeit small, of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  or Europe.  \par \par \uc2 "Recent U.S. data has been pretty good, so there may be an expectation that the payroll figures will shift the focus of the market to the relative strength of the U.S. economy," said Kikuko Takeda, market economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 At 0645 GMT, the dollar fetched around 103.55 yen, up from 103.25 in late U.S. trade and well off the five-year low of 101.83 hit on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro bought around $1.3275, little changed from late U.S. levels and down about a cent from the record high of 1.3385.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling stood at around $1.9220, compared with 1.9242 in late New York trade and the 12-year peak of 1.9440, also marked on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "Buy-backs of the dollar started yesterday and it continued well into today's Asian trade," said Shogo Nagaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. data for November is due at 1330 GMT. Economists' median forecast is for 180,000 new jobs compared with a rise of 337,000 in October.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar bulls hope a solid reading would cement the case for further rate hikes by the U.S. Federal Reserve, which would bolster the attraction of the dollar to foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 But others remained nervous, recalling that the dollar had failed to gain on surprisingly strong job figures for October, overwhelmed by concerns about the United States' ability to finance its gaping current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders said the market's reaction to the figures could serve as a litmus test for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "If the market sells the dollar even if the numbers are good, it'll be a sign that dollar sentiment is still bearish," said Katsunori Kitakura, manager of the treasury department at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 TIPTOES OVER \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Some traders said the dollar was also supported by caution over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese and European authorities, either alone or together.  \par \par \uc2 Officials from both regions have expressed concern that the dollar's rapid fall could damage their economies.  \par \par \uc2 Japan Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Thursday that Japan was in "close contact" with European and U.S. authorities due to the volatile market.  \par \par \uc2 But a senior euro zone official said later the same day that their finance ministers had not discussed joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki reiterated on Friday that the recent rise in the yen has been rapid and that Japan would take action if needed.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said on Thursday that recent moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were unwelcome. On \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said that such a weapon exists, but he would "never" comment further on it.  \par \par \uc2 Kitakura of Chuo Mitsui said: "We don't know if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Europe and will jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but both sets of officials have raised concern about the dollar's fall, so that's making the market nervous,"  \par \par \uc2 Traders were keen to hear from three members of the ECB's Governing Council due to speak later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 Austrian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Klaus Liebscher will speak at 0900 GMT and Belgian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Guy Quaden holds a news conference at 1030 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Later, Executive Board member Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell will talk about exchange rate movements between large economic areas at a Philadelphia Fed conference, where he will be joined by the bank's president Anthony Santomero and Boston Fed chief Cathy Minehan. (Additional reporting by Kazunori Takada).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041203e0c30060t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
