\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar builds on gains ahead of U.S. jobs data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 02:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 3 (Reuters) - The dollar edged up on Friday, building on a rebound from fresh lows the previous day, though investors stayed cautious ahead of closely watched U.S. jobs data.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on Thursday as traders locked in profits from a round of dollar-selling that drove the euro to record highs, the yen to a five-year peak and sterling to a 12-year high.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a sense of accomplishment in the market after the dollar fell to (those levels)," said Katsunori Kitakura, manager of the treasury department at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 "I want to see how the market reacts to the jobs data tonight. If the market sells the dollar even if the numbers are good, it'll be a sign that dollar sentiment is still bearish."  \par \par \uc2 At 0126 GMT, the dollar bought around 103.40 yen, up from 103.25 in late U.S. trade and well off the five-year low of 101.83 hit on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fetched around $1.3265, little changed from late U.S. levels but down more than a cent from the record high of 1.3385.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling stood at around $1.9215, compared with 1.9242 in late New York trade and the 12-year peak of 1.9440.  \par \par \uc2 The market was awaiting U.S. payrolls data for November due at 1330 GMT. Economists' median forecast is for 180,000 new jobs compared with a rise of 337,000 in October.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders said the dollar was also supported by caution over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese and European authorities.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Thursday that Japan was in "close contact" with European and U.S. authorities due to the volatile market.  \par \par \uc2 He reiterated on Friday that the recent rise in the yen has been rapid and that Japan would take action if needed.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said on Thursday that recent moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were unwelcome. On \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said that such a weapon exists, but would "never" comment further on it.  \par \par \uc2 A senior euro zone official said that European finance ministers had not discussed joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and had not urged the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We don't know if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Europe and will jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but both sets of officials have raised concerned about the dollar's fall so that's making the market nervous," said Kitakura of Chuo Mitsui.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041203e0c3005i9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
