\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Euro zone silent on report of  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  talks with Japan.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Wendel Broere   \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2004\par 13:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 UTRECHT, Netherlands, Dec 2 (Reuters) - Euro zone policymakers declined comment on Thursday on a report there had been talks with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on possible joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar, which has hit new lows against the euro and yen.   \par \par \uc2 But the chairman of the bloc's finance ministers said they would discuss at a meeting next week whether the euro's recent surge against the enfeebled dollar is damaging its economic prospects.   \par \par \uc2 The Financial Times said in a report that Japanese and euro zone authorities have discussed the prospect of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the dollar if it continues to tumble against the euro and yen.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki later declined to comment on possible concerted action, but told reporters in Tokyo: "We are in close contact with the euro authorities and the dollar authorities."   \par \par \uc2 He added that European authorities shared Toyko's concern.   \par \par \uc2 Dutch Finance Minister Gerrit Zalm, whose country holds the EU's rotating presidency, declined comment on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but said ministers would discuss the impact of the weak dollar at a scheduled meeting next week.   \par \par \uc2 Asked on the margins of a conference whether the level of the euro is harmful for the euro zone economy, Zalm told Reuters: "We will discuss that probably on Monday and Tuesday."   \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Vitor Constancio on Thursday was also non-committal on the FT report as he arrived at the ECB's Frankfurt headquarters for an interest rate-setting meeting.   \par \par \uc2 "No comment," the Portuguese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said in answer to reporters' questions.   \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet will face pressure for comments on the issue when he holds a post-meeting news conference at 1330 GMT to discuss what is expected to be a decision to keep the ECB's key interest rate at 2.0 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The euro hit a new high against the dollar above $1.3380 euro early on Thursday, but dropped back to around $1.3328 at 1150 GMT. The yen hit a five-year high at 101.86 per dollar before also losing some ground.   \par \par \uc2 BIG BATTLE   \par \par \uc2 "There is a big battle here between negative dollar sentiment and the fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or a big price correction, particularly given the speed with which the dollar has declined," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Mellon Bank.   \par \par \uc2 The United States has said it remains committed to a strong dollar but the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has declined rapidly since early September on worries over whether U.S. trade and budget deficits will prove sustainable.   \par \par \uc2 That has put pressure on euro zone exporters at a time when domestic demand is still shaky.   \par \par \uc2 Asked whether the United States' strong dollar policy was just rhetoric, Zalm said: "I think that what would be very helpful is that we have a credible commitment from the U.S. government to reduce the deficit in a faster way."   \par \par \uc2 Zalm said he hoped the Bush administration would come up with credible proposals to tackle its deficit problems.   \par \par \uc2 Asked what specific commitments he wanted to see from the United States, Zalm said: "To reduce the deficit and quickly, and not postponing it because that is at the heart of the problem."   \par \par \uc2 Zalm also said China may become more flexible about its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar in the future. That peg is seen as contributing to the U.S. current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 "They themselves say that they will be more flexible in the future, but they did not promise us anything about timing," Zalm said.   \par \par \uc2 "It would be helpful of course, because then the adjustment would not only be between the euro and dollar, but also between Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the dollar."    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041202e0c2007hp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
