\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar crumbles to record low vs euro.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs   \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2004\par 07:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The dollar sank to a record low against the euro on Thursday and stood near a 12-year low versus the pound after a round of robust U.S. economic data failed to dispel souring sentiment on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The dollar also held near multi-year lows against the yen, but the market was keeping an eye out for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy official, Hiroshi Watanabe, gave his clearest warning yet of possible dollar-buying action.   \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said on Wednesday that "conditions are in place" for Japan and Europe to act jointly to support the dollar and that other countries hurt by the falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could join in for any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 But traders have repeatedly driven the dollar lower despite verbal threats, waiting for evidence the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's near 9 percent drop against the euro and the yen in two months has gone too far too fast for Japanese and European officials.   \par \par \uc2 "Traders are not going to believe it until they see it. They're going to keep blowing up the dollar until actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  happens," said one trader at a European bank.   \par \par \uc2 In midday Tokyo trade, the euro was around $1.3360 , up from $1.3345 in late New York trade and just below the fresh record high of $1.3373.   \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed to around 102.35 yen per dollar , just off a 4-1/2-year high of 102.15 hit last week. Against the euro, it was stable at 136.80 yen .   \par \par \uc2 Reports showing accelerating U.S. factory growth in November and a strong start to consumer spending in October did nothing to stem the dollar's slide, despite bolstering expectations for the Federal Reserve to keep lifting official rates.   \par \par \uc2 By comparison, European data showed manufacturing growth stalled last month and new orders for goods contracting for the first time in more than a year, with some economists saying the dollar's gains were behind the dent to exports.   \par \par \uc2 Instead the market is focused almost exclusively on the United States' ability to fund its budget and current account deficits and suspicions that Washington wants a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help fix the trade imbalance. "Nobody seems to care about the economic data," said Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. "That indicates sentiment for the dollar is seriously, seriously bearish."   \par \par \uc2 Even a 1.5 percent jump in the S&P 500 on Monday to a new three-year high offered no solace for the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Underscoring the dollar's widespread woes, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed close to a 12-year low against the pound of $1.9359, trading at $1.9345 .   \par \par \uc2 That takes sterling back to levels against the dollar that were traded just before Britain was ejected from Europe's Exchange Rate Mechanism in 1992.   \par \par \uc2 TRICHET IN SPOTLIGHT   \par \par \uc2 Traders were looking to a news conference by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) chief Jean-Claude Trichet at 1330 GMT, following a monetary policy meeting, to see if he supports Watanabe's suggestion of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 If Trichet does not back up the idea of possible coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar will likely take another beating, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier this week Trichet indicated that the ECB would keep its policy stance of vigilance against inflation, comments traders viewed as suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to tolerate some euro strength.   \par \par \uc2 A stronger euro lessens the inflationary impact of higher energy costs.   \par \par \uc2 Traders and investors were also looking past the steady rise in U.S. government bond yields above those in Europe and well above those in Japan, though that rise could reflect investor diversification out of dollar-based assets.   \par \par \uc2 The difference between the 10-year U.S. Treasury note yield and the 10-year Bund is up almost 20 basis points in a little more than a week to 60 basis points, a four year high.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Fed has already doubled its funds rate this year to 2 percent and is expected to raise by another quarter-percentage point at its mid-December meeting and following meetings.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041202e0c2004n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
