en per dollar, just off a 4-1/2-year high of 102.15 hit last week. Against the euro, it was stable at 136.80 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Reports showing accelerating U.S. factory growth in November and a strong start to consumer spending in October did nothing to stem the dollar's slide, despite bolstering expectations for the Federal Reserve to keep lifting official rates.  \par \par \uc2 By comparison, European data showed manufacturing growth stalled last month and new orders for goods contracting for the first time in more than a year, with some economists saying the dollar's gains were behind the dent to exports.  \par \par \uc2 Instead the market is focused almost exclusively on the United States' ability to fund its budget and current account deficits and suspicions that Washington wants a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help fix the trade imbalance. "Nobody seems to care about the economic data," said Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. "That indicates sentiment for the dollar is seriously, seriously bearish."  \par \par \uc2 Even a 1.5 percent jump in the S&P 500 on Monday to a new three-year high offered no solace for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Underscoring the dollar's widespread woes, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed close to a 12-year low against the pound of $1.9359, trading at $1.9345.  \par \par \uc2 That takes sterling back to levels against the dollar that were traded just before Britain was ejected from Europe's Exchange Rate Mechanism in 1992.  \par \par \uc2 TRICHET IN SPOTLIGHT  \par \par \uc2 Traders were looking to a news conference by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) chief Jean-Claude Trichet at 1330 GMT, following a monetary policy meeting, to see if he supports Watanabe's suggestion of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 If Trichet does not back up the idea of possible coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar will likely take another beating, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this week Trichet indicated that the ECB would keep its policy stance of vigilance against inflation, comments traders viewed as suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to tolerate some euro strength.  \par \par \uc2 A stronger euro lessens the inflationary impact of higher energy costs.  \par \par \uc2 Traders and investors were also looking past the steady rise in U.S. government bond yields above those in Europe and well above those in Japan, though that rise could reflect investor diversification out of dollar-based assets.  \par \par \uc2 The difference between the 10-year U.S. Treasury note yield and the 10-year Bund is up almost 20 basis points in a little more than a week to 60 basis points, a four year high.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Fed has already doubled its funds rate this year to 2 percent and is expected to raise by another quarter-percentage point at its mid-December meeting and following meetings.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041202e0c20038s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
