\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rallies off new lows on profit-taking binge.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrea Ricci  \par \uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2004\par 20:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The dollar rallied on Thursday as fears of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a sharp fall in oil prices convinced traders that it was a good time to take profits after days of heavy dollar sales.  \par \par \uc2 After registering another all-time low against the euro overnight, the dollar rose as high as $1.3239 per euro. The dollar had set new lows against the euro in seven of the last eight sessions.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback also rose from a five-year low against the yen and a new 12-year bottom against sterling.  \par \par \uc2 Richard Franulovich, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac Banking Corp. in New York, said selling of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the dollar "cascaded" after the dollar made new lows.  \par \par \uc2 Fears about dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the desire to cut back on short dollar positions ahead of Friday's U.S. payrolls report encouraged traders to buy back dollars, Franulovich said.  \par \par \uc2 "People are nervous about the BOJ and there was a big washout of dollar shorts," he said.  \par \par \uc2 In early afternoon New York trading, the euro was down 0.40 percent at $1.3274, far below the record high of $1.3383 touched in overnight trade, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was up 0.39 percent at 103.02 yen, well off a five-year low of 101.86 yen reached earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was up 0.85 percent at 1.1500 francs.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was down 0.51 percent to $1.9229 after earlier posting a new 12-year high of $1.9438.  \par \par \uc2 Japan on Thursday said that its concern about the dollar's steep decline was shared by Europe and the United States, keeping the market guessing about possible concerted action.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking just after the dollar had fallen below 102 yen for the first time since January 2000, Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said market moves had been volatile and Japan was in "close contact" with European and U.S. authorities.  \par \par \uc2 But a senior euro zone official later said that European finance ministers had not discussed joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and did not urge the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 OIL EFFECT  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said that a second day of sharp falls in oil prices also helped boost the dollar on views lower prices would aid the U.S. economy and lessen inflationary pressure.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices fell more than 5 percent on Thursday and U.S. light sweet crude prices are now about $6 lower than just two days ago at $43.25.  \par \par \uc2 "The fall in the oil price is one of the catalysts for the rebound in the dollar," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York.  \par \par \uc2 But he called the dollar's rise "just a short-term move as some of the speculative money has come off the table."  \par \par \uc2 TJ Marta, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with RBC Capital Markets in New York, also said he expected the dollar's rebound to be short-lived.  \par \par \uc2 "What we are seeing today is an unwinding of leveraged positions, but many real money names and longer-term model names - any money that has more than a one-week time horizon - are in selling the dollar," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market showed a muted reaction to U.S. economic data on Thursday. Factory orders for October rose a more-than-expected 0.5 percent on demand for military aircraft, but a revision to October durable goods orders showed a big decline.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. will release November payrolls data on Friday, and Marta said a strong number could spur further short-term dollar gains. Economists' median forecast is for a rise of 180,000.  \par \par \uc2 "If we happen to get an above consensus number tomorrow, it perhaps takes the dollar higher," he said. "But the overriding concern with the U.S. economy is structural, and it is hard to make cyclical arguments for the dollar."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041202e0c200aq5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
