\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: BOJ To Ease Policy Next Year - UFJ Shimanaka  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hiroshi Inoue  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2004\par 05:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan will likely ease policy next year, under pressure from a faltering economy, which peaked in the middle of this year, says Yuji Shimanaka, chief economist and general manager of investment research UFJ Institute.  \par \par \uc2 While many economists ponder the likely timing of an end to the BOJ's ultra-loose quantitative easing, Shimanaka says the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't have an opportunity to alter the policy for at least another two years.  \par \par \uc2 "Various leading indicators have worsened and signal that the nation's economy has already entered a down trend," Shimanaka told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. "Pressure on the BOJ to easy policy will increase next year."  \par \par \uc2 The economist expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase the upper end of its target range for liquidity, which is measured by commercial banks' account balances at the BOJ, to Y37 trillion or Y38 trillion from Y35 trillion.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ's existing target is Y30 trillion to Y35 trillion.  \par \par \uc2 Shimanaka doesn't expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise the lower end of the target as recently the BOJ has had "difficulty in injecting liquidity due to weak fund demand by financial institutions."  \par \par \uc2 It is possible that the BOJ will increase by Y200 billion its monthly outright buying of Japanese government bond buying to Y1.4 trillion, he added. Although it is also possible that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't alter its JGB purchases. Since BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui became governor in March 2003, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased policy a number of times but held steady its JGB purchase amount.  \par \par \uc2 "I had initially expected the BOJ to end the quantitative easing between July and September of 2006, but I now expect the easy policy to end in the October-December quarter of 2007," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Shimanaka expects the BOJ to hold back from ending the easy policy until after the government has increased its consumption tax, which may not occur until sometime during or after fiscal 2007, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Dollar To Fall Below Y100 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shimanaka noted that the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development's economic leading indicator peaked in March and has fallen since then. The indicator points to output in OECD nations six months ahead.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Latest data on industrial production for Japan issued Tuesday showed output slumped 1.6% in October from September.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's real gross domestic product is likely to grow by just 0.7% next fiscal year starting April 2005 and once the Cabinet Office adopts its planned new method of calculating GDP, that forecast may fall to 0.3%, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The new method, which Cabinet Office plans to introduce from Dec. 8, will reduce the size of fall in the GDP deflator, which in turn will reduce real GDP growth rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shimanaka expects a stronger yen against the dollar to weigh on Japan's economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The yen's appreciation will likely continue with the dollar set to break below Y100 and possibly to Y95," he said. "Even if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  alone conducts yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the action wouldn't have an effect on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan will find it a lonely battle to stop the yen from rising as the U.S. appears willing to let the dollar weaken to help it reduce its large current account deficit, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yen strength will hurt sentiment among firms and consumers but the government has little ability to ease fiscal policy to help the economy as the government's budget is already sharply in deficit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shimanaka said the BOJ needs to increase the monetary base to tackle a strong yen as high growth in the U.S. monetary base is one factor behind the recent fall in the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's monetary base likely rose 4.9% in November from a year ago, below growth in the U.S. monetary base of around 5.7%, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If economic text books are any guide, increasing monetary base growth above the U.S. level is necessary for the BOJ to curb a stronger yen," Shimanaka said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The stronger yen will help keep a lid on consumer prices, he said. Shimanaka expects the core consumer price index to rise 0.1% next fiscal year although risks to that forecast are on the downside.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ board members also forecast last month a 0.1% rise in the core CPI.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Hiroshi Inoue, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2995; Hiroshi.Inoue@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Bennett Richardson [ 30-11-04 0413GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041130e0bu0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
