\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar resumes downtrend, awaits Trichet, U.S. data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Hunt  \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2004\par 14:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 30 (Reuters) - The dollar resumed its downtrend on Tuesday, approaching within half a cent of last week's record low versus the euro as selling gathered steam before comments from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief and U.S. economic data.   \par \par \uc2  The greenback had clawed back some ground against the yen after data suggested Japanese economic growth had slowed, but dollar bears reemerged after sterling clocked a nine-month high against dollar after upbeat UK data.   \par \par \uc2  "There has been some doubt over the past few days that the big trend up (in euro/dollar) had come to an end. But as cable (sterling/dollar) hit new highs it reassured people that the dollar was still under pressure," said Lee Ferridge, senior proprietary trader at Rabobank.   \par \par \uc2  Since mid-October, the dollar has lost seven percent against the yen and the euro on concerns about the U.S. current account deficit and speculation Washington is happy to see a weaker dollar.   \par \par \uc2  By 1250 GMT, the dollar stood at $1.3295 per euro, down 0.15 percent and within sight of Friday's record low beyond $1.33.   \par \par \uc2  Against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it stood at 102.60 yen, reversing earlier gains and homing in on a 4-1/2 year low of 102.12 yen hit last week.   \par \par \uc2  Sterling hit a nine-month high of $1.9090 after strong UK housing data and comments from Bank of England Governor Mervyn King, who said inflation was seen rising above target in two years.   \par \par \uc2  TRICHET, US DATA   \par \par \uc2  ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet, who has warned recent euro moves were "brutal" and "unwelcome", is testifying before the European Parliament from 1400 GMT.   \par \par \uc2  A preliminary reading of third-quarter U.S. growth, Chicago manufacturing activity data and consumer confidence figures from the Conference Board for November are also due at 1500 GMT.   \par \par \uc2  "Consumer confidence - people are expecting a bounce and if the data confirms that it could be a bullish dollar signal," said Daragh Maher, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Calyon.   \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expect a reading of 96.5 compared with 92.8 in October.   \par \par \uc2  But some traders said that strong U.S. indicators could fail to lift the dollar, noting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell sharply even after surprisingly strong U.S. payroll data earlier this month.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar gained ground in Asian trade after Japan's industrial production fell 1.6 percent in October from a month earlier, well below market expectations for a gain of 0.1 percent.   \par \par \uc2  Signs are emerging that a recovery in the world's second-largest economy is losing momentum as the global economy slows down and a higher yen is threatening to damage exports.   \par \par \uc2  There were also concerns about euro zone economic growth, after European Commission data showing economic sentiment fell to a four-month low in November as confidence in the service sector took a hit.   \par \par \uc2  Concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise, also put the dollar on a firmer footing earlier in the session.   \par \par \uc2  However, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said earlier on Tuesday it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in November. It last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March.   \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar was the worst performer of the day after an unexpected decline in October retail sales and building data suggested interest rates will stay on hold. It fell to a two-week low of US$0.7696.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041130e0bu00842\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
