\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar holds ground, waits for Trichet, U.S. data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Hunt  \par \uc2 500 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2004\par 12:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 30 (Reuters) - The dollar clawed back some ground against the yen on Tuesday after data suggested that Japanese economic growth had slowed, but remained within sight of record lows against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Markets were cautious ahead of testimony from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief and U.S. economic data but most analysts said that dollar gains were likely to be short-lived.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we've moved into a period of consolidation. I think to some extent we've had such a swift run that we are having a pause here," said Daragh Maher, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Calyon. "I don't think there's been a underlying shift in fundamentals. People are still dollar negative."  \par \par \uc2 Since mid-October, the dollar has lost seven percent against the yen and the euro on concerns about the U.S. current account deficit and speculation Washington is happy to see a weaker dollar.  \par \par \uc2 By 1100 GMT, the dollar stood at $1.3260 per euro, virtually unchanged on the day after hitting a record low on Friday beyond $1.33.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it stood at 102.95 yen, up 0.12 percent on the day and above a 4-1/2 year low of 102.12 yen hit last week.  \par \par \uc2 SLOWING ECONOMY  \par \par \uc2 Japan's industrial production fell 1.6 percent in October from a month earlier, well below market expectations for a gain of 0.1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Signs are emerging that a recovery in the world's second-largest economy is losing momentum as the global economy slows down and a higher yen is threatening to damage exports.  \par \par \uc2 There were also concerns about euro zone economic growth, after European Commission data showing economic sentiment fell to a four-month low in November as confidence in the service sector took a hit.  \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet, who has warned recent euro moves were "brutal" and "unwelcome", is testifying before the European Parliament from 1400 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 A preliminary reading of third-quarter U.S. growth, Chicago manufacturing activity data and consumer confidence figures are also due.  \par \par \uc2 "We are also seeing initial signs a stronger euro is having an impact on the euro zone economy. Trichet is going to divert little from his recent comments. Given this is a corrective week for the dollar, if any data is going to have an impact, this will be the week," said Adam Myers, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Societe Generale.  \par \par \uc2 Concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise, also put the dollar on a firmer footing.  \par \par \uc2 However, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said earlier on Tuesday it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in November. It last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was the worst performer of the day after an unexpected decline in October retail sales and building data suggested interest rates will stay on hold. It fell to a two-week low of US$0.7696.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041130e0bu0079q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
