\par\par \b \uc2 DJ US Stocks Up On Strong Post-Thanksgiving Shopping Sales  \b0\par\par\uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 29, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 A WALL STREET JOURNAL ONLINE NEWS ROUNDUP  \par \par \uc2 Stocks are up Monday as hopes for strong holiday sales may help investors snap the post-Thanksgiving holiday turkey funk. Recently, the Dow Jones Industrial Average was up 23 points to 10545, the Nasdaq was higher by 11 to 2113 and the Standard & Poor's 500 was up 2 to 1185. Early reports for retail sales on the Friday after Thanksgiving, typically one of the busiest shopping days of the year, were an encouraging sign as the holiday shopping season kicks into high gear. Retail-tracking firm ShopperTrak estimated that retail sales for "Black Friday," so-called because it marks a shift to profitability for many retailers, rose 10.8% over last year to hit $8 billion. Retail giant Wal-Mart Stores, however, put a bit of a damper on the festivities, slashing its prediction for November sales growth to 0.7%, far less than the 2% to 4% growth many analysts had expected. Mall cornerstones J.C. Penney and Sears Roebuck also remained cautious about overall holiday sales, despite the robust customer turnout this weekend. Wal-Mart hares were off 3.1%, Sears was off 1.6% and J.C. Penney was down 0.4%. Apple Computer shares jumped 6.4% after three brokerage firms raised their earnings estimates and price targets for the company on expectations that iPods will be a hot item this holiday season. UBS analyst Ben Reitzes, who raised his price target on Apple to $77 from $66, wrote in a research note Monday that the "iPod buzz helps drive a surge in retail sales and profits as Apple proves itself as a high-end retailer." In other corporate news, International Business Machines, up 1.6% on Monday, unveiled a chip that is expected to give microprocessor king Intel strong competition in the living rooms of the future. IBM has been working with Japanese electronics giants Sony and Toshiba for four years on a chip design, code-named "Cell." The three companies said the chip will go into limited production next year and will be used in several consumer-product applications starting in 2006. No U.S. economic data are due out Monday. The dollar rose to Y102.89 after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki repeated that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Meanwhile, the euro slipped to US$1.3257 after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said the European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was "unwelcome." In Tokyo, the Nikkei 225 Stock Average jumped 1.3% to close at 10977.89 as investors were encouraged by the dollar's resurgence against the yen. The gains in Japan helped buoy European stocks at midday. In London, the Financial Times Stock Exchange 100-share index rose 0.8%, with an added boost of better-than-expected United Kingdom consumer-confidence data for November, "which is slightly surprising given the weakness of housing market," said Philip Shaw, an analyst at Investec. In Frankfurt, the benchmark Xetra Dax shot up 1.3%. On Friday, U.S. stocks finished the holiday-shortened session flat. The Dow industrial average gained 1.92 points to close at 10522.23, while the S&P 500 rose 0.89 to 1182.65 and the Nasdaq Composite Index lost 0.57 to 2101.57. This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal Web site, WSJ.com.)  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \