\par\par \b \uc2 DJ WSJ.COM: US Stocks Seen Higher On Strong Holiday Sales  \b0\par\par\uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 29, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 A WALL STREET JOURNAL ONLINE NEWS ROUNDUP  \par \par \uc2 Hopes for a merry retail Christmas may help investors snap the post-turkey funk Monday as many return to work after a long holiday weekend. Recently, futures on the Standard & Poor's 500 Index were up 6.40 to 1187.20, Dow Jones Industrial Average futures rose by 54 to 10564 and Nasdaq futures were up 15 to 1592.50. Early reports for retail sales on the Friday after Thanksgiving, typically one of the busiest shopping days of the year, were an encouraging sign as the holiday shopping season kicks into high gear. Retail-tracking firm ShopperTrak estimated that retail sales for "Black Friday," so-called because it marks a shift to profitability for many retailers, rose 10.8% over last year to hit $8 billion. Retail giant Wal-Mart Stores, however, put a bit of a damper on the party at the cash register, slashing its prediction for November sales growth to 0.7%, far less than the 2% to 4% growth many analysts had expected. Mall cornerstones J.C. Penney and Sears Roebuck also remained cautious about overall holiday sales, despite dazzling customer turnout this weekend. In other corporate news, International Business Machines on Monday unveiled a chip that is expected to give microprocessor king Intel strong competition in the living rooms of the future. IBM has been working with Japanese electronics giants Sony and Toshiba for four years on a chip design, code-named "Cell." The three companies said the chip will go into limited production next year and will be used in several consumer-product applications starting in 2006. The dollar rose to Y102.89 after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki repeated that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Meanwhile, the euro slipped to US$1.3257 after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said the European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was "unwelcome." In Tokyo, the Nikkei 225 Stock Average jumped 1.3% to close at 10977.89 as investors were encouraged by the dollar's resurgence against the yen. The gains in Japan helped buoy European stocks at midday. In London, the Financial Times Stock Exchange 100-share index rose 0.8%, with an added boost of better-than-expected United Kingdom consumer-confidence data for November, "which is slightly surprising given the weakness of housing market," said Philip Shaw, an analyst at Investec. In Frankfurt, the benchmark Xetra Dax shot up 1.4%. The biggest gainer, HVB Group, rose 2.9% following reports of changes in management and planned cost cuts. Persistent strong demand for steel buoyed ThyssenKrupp, which advanced 1.7%. On Friday, U.S. stocks finished the holiday-shortened session flat. The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 1.92 points to close at 10522.23, while the Standard & Poor's 500-stock index rose 0.89 to 1182.65 and the Nasdaq Composite Index lost 0.57 to 2101.57. In Friday's trading: In Europe, London's FTSE 100 fell amid a new surge in the euro and concerns about holiday spending in the U.S. In Asia, Tokyo's Nikkei 225 slipped 0.61% to 10833.75, while markets in Australia and Indonesia hit records. Stocks rose in the U.S. On the New York Stock Exchange, where 504.4 million shares traded, 1,845 stocks rose and 1,231 fell. On the Nasdaq Stock Market, where volume was 665.8 million, 1,705 issues advanced and 1,218 declined. Bonds dropped. The 10-year Treasury note fell 11/32, or $3.44 for each $1,000 invested. The yield, which moves inversely to price, rose to 4.240%. The 30-year note lost 22/32 to yield 4.890%. The dollar was lower. It traded at Y102.58, down from Y102.82, while the euro rose against the dollar to $1.3292 from $1.3177. For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041129e0bt004hm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
