\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  may  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  soon to prop up dollar-report.   \b0\par\par\uc2 278 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2004\par 21:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Nov 29 (Reuters) - Japanese monetary authorities may be spurred to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar once the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls below 102 yen, according to a report from New York-based consultants Medley Global Advisors.    \par \par \uc2  A market source who read the report said it cited a conversation last Friday between Japanese officials and business leaders in Japan on the dollar/yen exchange rate.    \par \par \uc2  The officials said they were determined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  without consulting other countries if the dollar dropped below recent lows.    \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a fresh 4-1/2-year low against the yen  on Friday, at 102.12 yen, according to Reuters data.    \par \par \uc2  The source said that the report suggested that Japan could possibly step in even before the dollar reaches 102 yen.    \par \par \uc2  A stronger yen makes Japanese exports more expensive overseas.    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy dollars since March this year.    \par \par \uc2  Japan, the world's second-largest economy, spent a record 20 trillion yen ($194 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and 15 trillion yen ($146 billion) in the first quarter of this year.    \par \par \uc2  The Medley report, according to a market source, also said senior Chinese officials recently expressed their concerns with an official from the U.S. Treasury Department over the dollar's recent decline. The report said Chinese officials had said Beijing may have to act on its own if the dollar continues to decline. The report interpreted that as a threat by China to curb purchases of U.S. Treasury debt.    \par \par \uc2  China's Treasury holdings in September stood at $174 billion.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041129e0bt00dl3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
