\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rests after sharp losses, still weak.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2004\par 14:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 29 (Reuters) - The dollar held above last week's record low against the euro and 4-1/2 year lows against the yen on Monday, winning some respite after 10 consecutive weeks of losses.  \par \par \uc2 Signs that China is in no rush to let its pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate against the dollar gave some support to the greenback which has shed nearly nine percent against the euro and the yen in the last two months.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking on Sunday, Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao played down the likelihood of a near-term yuan revaluation and questioned why the United States had taken no action to stem the dollar's fall.  \par \par \uc2 In Europe, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet renewed warnings against a stronger euro, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent movements were not welcome.  \par \par \uc2 However, analysts said the dollar's gains on Monday were only a pause in its long-term downtrend and had been driven mainly by technical factors.  \par \par \uc2 "We set a new high (in euro/dollar) on Friday and the market is a bit exhausted and overdone," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 "But everybody is still very negative on the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up 0.4 percent on the day at 102.90 yen and 0.4 percent up at $1.3235 per euro in European trade.  \par \par \uc2 "The $1.32 area should be the first test but I don't expect anything big," said Fontaine.  \par \par \uc2 OFFICIAL DEMAND  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled to a record low against the euro, at $1.3329, and multi-year lows against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday after a report that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had cut back on U.S. Treasuries in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser quoted in the report later said he had no knowledge of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions and the dollar pulled up from its lows.  \par \par \uc2 But the report played on one of the market's biggest fears - that without purchases of dollar assets by central banks, the United States may find it difficult or even impossible to finance its large current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "The comments from China have given the dollar some support against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the bear trend is very much in place," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 "Everyone is focused on central banks. Any hint they are changing the composition of their reserves could put renewed pressure on the dollar," said Mackel.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese data showing a big decline in retail sales in October had little market impact. Instead, market players are keeping an eye out for signs that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is preparing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the yen's export-damaging rise against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's vice finance minister Koichi Hosokawa repeated his warnings against the yen's recent rise, saying authorities would act on rapid moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 UBS analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a "big worry", but that the yen's gains had been mostly fuelled by speculative bets rather than investment flows into Japan, potentially limiting any further appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 SWISS PRESSURE  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc came under pressure after the chairman of the Swiss National Bank (SNB) signalled the bank may be reluctant to raise interest rates for a third time this year next month.  \par \par \uc2 "Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market developments show that in highly turbulent times the Swiss franc can come under upward speculative pressure," SNB chief Jean-Pierre Roth said in a speech prepared for delivery at the 50th anniversary of the Bank of Israel in Jerusalem.  \par \par \uc2 "This may interfere with our domestic monetary strategy," he added.  \par \par \uc2 His remarks prompted the Swiss franc, which has been trading at 9-year highs against the dollar, to fall to 1.1467 before paring losses.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041129e0bt009qf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
