\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar teeters above lows as central banks eyed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2004\par 10:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 29 (Reuters) - The dollar teetered above last week's record low against the euro and 4-1/2 year low against the yen on Monday as traders afforded the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  some respite after 10 consecutive weeks of losses.  \par \par \uc2 Signs China is in no rush to let its pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate against the dollar gave some support to the greenback which has shed nearly 9 percent against the euro and the yen in the last two months.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking on Sunday, Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao played down the likelihood of a yuan revaluation in the near term and questioned why U.S. policymakers had taken no action to stem the dollar's fall.  \par \par \uc2 "The comments from China have given the dollar some support against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the bear trend is very much in place," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was up nearly half a percent at 102.98 yen  and a\par quarter-percent $1.3260 per euro        in early European trade.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tumbled to a record low against the euro, at $1.3329, and multi-year lows against most major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Friday after a report that China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had cut back on U.S. Treasuries in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adviser quoted in the report later said he had no knowledge of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  actions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the report heightened one of the market's biggest fears - that central banks are becoming wary of funding the record U.S. current account deficit and holding dollar investments.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Worries about the United States' ability to fund its current account and budget deficits have driven the dollar repeatedly lower in recent weeks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Everyone is focused on central banks. Any hint they are changing the composition of their reserves could put renewed pressure on the dollar," said Mackel.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese data showing a big decline in retail sales in October had little market impact. Instead, market players are keeping an eye out for signs that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is preparing to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cap the yen's export-damaging strength against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister, Sadakazu Tanigaki, repeated on Monday that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves needed to be watched closely and that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would take decisive action when needed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UBS analysts said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was a "big worry", but that the yen's gains had been mostly fuelled by speculative bets rather than investment flows into Japan, potentially limiting any further appreciation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  president Jean-Claude Trichet speaking in Vienna on Monday reiterated his stance that recent movements in the euro were not welcome.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swiss franc came under pressure after the chairman of the Swiss National Bank signalled the bank may be reluctant to raise interest rates for a third time this year next month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market developments show that in highly turbulent times the Swiss franc can come under upward speculative pressure," SNB chief Jean-Pierre Roth said in a speech prepared for delivery at the 50th anniversary of the Bank of Israel in Jerusalem.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This may interfere with our domestic monetary strategy," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 His remarks prompted the Swiss franc, which has been trading at 9-year highs against the dollar, to fall to 1.1459 before paring losses.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041129e0bt007n6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
