\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar stuck near lows, central banks eyed.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs   \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2004\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 29 (Reuters) - The dollar hovered near a record low against the euro and a 4-1/2-year low against the yen on Monday as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained vulnerable to worries about major central banks trimming dollar assets.    \par \par \uc2  But profit-taking on short positions gave a small lift to the dollar, which has shed nearly 9 percent against the euro and yen in two months. Market players were also keeping an eye out for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people are nervous and quick to take profits," said one head \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a European bank.    \par \par \uc2  The dollar's latest woes came on Friday from a report, later denied, that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had cut back on U.S. Treasuries in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.    \par \par \uc2  The report heightened one of the market's biggest fears: that Asian central banks in particular are becoming wary of funding the record U.S. current account deficit and holding dollar investments.    \par \par \uc2  Worries about the United States' ability to fund its current account and budget deficits have driven the dollar repeatedly lower in recent weeks, while investors have bet Washington prefers a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help resolve its imbalances.    \par \par \uc2  "The market is focused squarely on further dollar downside," Singapore-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at UBS Investment Bank said in a note to clients on Monday.    \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, speculation that China may soon loosen the peg of its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar and let it appreciate has fuelled dollar losses against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \uc2  Such speculation has mounted before an upcoming meeting of top Chinese economic officials and as the dollar's fall has accelerated.    \par \par \uc2  But Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao played down the likelihood of a yuan revaluation coming in the near term, saying over the weekend that China needed a stable economy and healthy financial system to unshackle the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.    \par \par \uc2  Wen also said China was unlikely to make such a move amid intense speculation, a statement repeated by China's vice \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Li Ruogu on Monday, according to the China News Service.    \par \par \uc2  In midday Tokyo trade, the dollar  had recovered to $1.3255 against the euro compared with Friday's record low of $1.3330. The dollar hit consecutive record lows against the euro in the four prior trading sessions.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the yen  the dollar traded near 102.90 yen - up from Friday's\par 102.15 yen, the lowest since March 2000. The dollar had traded up to\par 103.45 yen on Friday before resuming its downward slide. The yen\par remained in a range against the euro          , trading just under\par 136.35 yen.\par    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WATCH\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese data showing a big decline in retail sales in October had no\par impact. Instead, market players are keeping an eye out for sustained\par profit-taking that could boost the dollar even more, as well as\par possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister, Sadakazu Tanigaki, repeated on Monday his\par warning that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements needed to be watched closely\par and that Japan would take decisive action when needed, but traders\par increasingly view such remarks as hollow.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The UBS analysts said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was a "big worry", but that the\par yen's gains had been mostly fuelled by speculative bets rather than\par investment flows into Japan, potentially limiting any further\par appreciation.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders were still mulling Friday's report by a Shanghai business\par newspaper that quoted a policy board member of China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as\par saying it had started cutting its dollar \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves,\par which the paper cited at $180 billion.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That number, however, was tough to square with available data.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Treasury figures showed Chinese investors holding $174 billion of\par U.S. Treasuries in September, with the bulk likely held by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041129e0bt00699\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
