\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian stocks climb as dollar steadies.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Callus  \par \uc2 866 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2004\par 07:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 29 (Reuters) - The dollar clawed above last week's record low against the euro on Monday and firmed against the yen, allowing shipping and steel shares to boost Asian stock markets.   \par \par \uc2  Gold edged back from last week's 16-1/4-year highs to stand at $451.50 an ounce and January NYMEX crude oil was down 22 cents at $49.22 a barrel after a four-day holiday weekend closure in New York.   \par \par \uc2  European stocks were expected to open weaker amid worries over the dollar and oil prices. Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei average closed up 1.33 percent at 10,977.89 and an MSCI index of non-Japan shares was up 0.49 percent, standing just below its highest level in nearly 5 years.   \par \par \uc2  "It's probably premature to say the yen's rally is over, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is calm today and that's helped to ease one big worry hanging over the stock market," said Masatoshi Sato, an equity strategist at Mizuho Investors Securities.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said profit-taking on short positions gave a small lift to the dollar, which has shed nearly 9 percent against the euro and yen in 2 months. Market players were also keeping an eye out for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  The dollar's latest woes came on Friday from a report, later denied, that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut back on U.S. Treasuries in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar recovered to $1.3274 against the euro compared with Friday's record low of $1.3330. The dollar has hit a record low against the euro for four consecutive trading sessions.   \par \par \uc2  Against the yen the dollar traded at 102.80 yen - up from Friday's 102.15 yen, the lowest since March 2000. The dollar had traded all the way back up to 103.45 yen on profit-taking before resuming its downward slide.   \par \par \uc2  HOLLOW REMARKS   \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki repeated on Monday his warning that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements need to be watched closely and that Japan will take decisive action when needed, but traders increasingly view such remarks as hollow.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers once again looked past economic data. Japanese retail sales fell 1.4 percent in October, much worse than forecast, though the figures were distorted by the effect of typhoons and a deadly earthquake during the month.   \par \par \uc2  Speculation that China could soon loosen the peg of its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar and let it appreciate has fuelled dollar losses against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  But Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao played down the likelihood of a yuan revaluation coming soon, saying over the weekend that China needed a stable economy and healthy financial system to unshackle the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.   \par \par \uc2  SHIPPING STRONG, STEELS GLOW   \par \par \uc2  Shipping firms including Nippon Yusen KK, up 2.4 percent, gained after dry cargo freight rates hit an all-time high last week. Japan's biggest trading house, Mitsubishi Corp., led trading stocks up after investor Jim Rogers talked up a recent bull run in commodities prices.   \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's steel sector index ISTEL.firmed 1.5 percent, extending last week's climb on expectations of higher prices, and Taiwan's steel index climbed 1.7 percent. South Korea's POSCO, the world's most profitable listed steel firm, was up 2.1 percent. Misawa Homes Holdings Inc. soared 9.9 percent after financial sources said UFJ Holdings Inc would ask Toyota Motor Corp to invest in and back the restructuring of the troubled home builder.   \par \par \uc2  Australian steelmaker OneSteel missed out on global steel sector euphoria, slipping 3.8 percent on news of production problems at a blast furnace, but Australian retail stocks were up on hopes for bumper Christmas sales. The S&P ASX 200 touched another record high at 3.933.3 before closing up 0.4 percent at 3,926.8.   \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, Samsung shares dipped 1.6 percent to a 1-month low after key investment banks cut their earnings forecasts and their share target price.   \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng was up 1.42 percent at 14,092.86 with Hutchison Whampoa Ltd. a top gainer, rising 4.2 percent on talk that its partly owned Canadian oil unit, Husky Energy, may be sold to a Chinese oil firm.   \par \par \uc2  Singapore's Straits Times climbed 0.62 percent, Taiwan's benchmark index added 0.11 percent and South Korea's KOSPI rose 0.85 percent.   \par \par \uc2  World stocks as measured by the MSCI were up 0.14 percent, just below a 3-year high.   \par \par \uc2  December 10-year JGB futures slipped 0.25 points to 138.55 in reaction to the stock market's climb and on caution ahead of a 1.9 trillion yen auction of 10-year JGBs on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  On Friday, in quiet trade after Thursday's Thanksgiving holiday, the Dow Jones industrial average closed up 0.02 percent at 10,522.23, with steel stocks leading gains.   \par \par \uc2  The Standard & Poor's 500 Index put on 0.08 percent to 1,182.65 and the Nasdaq Composite Index edged 0.03 percent lower to 2,101.97.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041129e0bt00693\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
