\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian stocks edge up as dollar steadies.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Callus  \par \uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2004\par 04:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 29 (Reuters) - The dollar strengthened above last week's record low against the euro on Monday, allowing shares in shippers and steelmakers to take Asian equity markets up, but nerves about the dollar's direction capped stock gains.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gold  edged back from last week's 16-1/4-year highs to stand at $450.75\par an ounce and January NYMEX crude oil        opened slightly weaker\par after a four-day holiday weekend closure in New York, down 16 cents at\par $49.28 a barrel. Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei average         rose 1.42\par percent to 10,987.71 in morning trade and an MSCI index of non-Japan\par shares            edged up 0.39 percent to stand just below its highest\par level in nearly 5 years.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2  "A pause in the yen's rally gave a cue to the market," said Ken Masuda, a senior dealer in Japanese equities at Shinko Securities.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said profit-taking on short positions gave a small lift to the dollar, which has shed nearly 9 percent against the euro and yen in two months. Market players were also keeping an eye out for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  The dollar's latest woes came on Friday from a report, later denied, that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut back on U.S. Treasuries in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.   \par \par \uc2  "The market is focused squarely on further dollar downside," Singapore-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at UBS Investment Bank said in a note to clients on Monday.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar recovered to $1.3252 against the euro  compared with Friday's record low of $1.3330. The dollar has hit a record low against the euro for four consecutive trading sessions.   \par \par \uc2  Against the yen , the dollar traded at 102.90 yen - up from Friday's 102.15 yen, the lowest since March 2000. The dollar had traded all the way back up to 103.45 yen on profit-taking before resuming its downward slide.   \par \par \uc2  HOLLOW REMARKS   \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki repeated on Monday his warning that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements need to be watched closely and that Japan will take decisive action when needed, but traders increasingly view such remarks as hollow.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers once again looked past economic data. Japanese retail sales fell 1.4 percent in October, much worse than forecast, though the figures were distorted by the effect of typhoons and a deadly earthquake during the month.   \par \par \uc2  Speculation that China could soon loosen the peg of its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar and let it appreciate has fuelled dollar losses against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  But Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao played down the likelihood of a yuan revaluation coming soon, saying over the weekend that China needed a stable economy and healthy financial system to unshackle the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.   \par \par \uc2  SHIPPING STRONG   \par \par \uc2  Shipping firms including Nippon Yusen KK (9101.T), up 2.6 percent, gained after dry cargo freight rates  hit an all-time high last week.   \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's steel sector index  firmed 1.4 percent, extending last week's climb on expectations of higher prices.   \par \par \uc2  Misawa Homes Holdings Inc. (1722.T) soared 9.2 percent after financial sources said UFJ Holdings Inc (8307.T) would ask Toyota Motor Corp (7203.T) to invest in and back the restructuring of the troubled home builder.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hong Kong's Hang Seng  was up 1.33 percent at 14,080.36. Hutchison\par Whampoa Ltd. (0013.HK) was a top gainer, rising 3.4 percent on talk\par that its partly owned Canadian oil unit, Husky Energy          , may be\par sold to a Chinese oil firm.\par Singapore's Straits Times  climbed 0.35 percent, Taiwan's benchmark\par index         added 0.38 percent and South Korea's KOSPI         rose\par 1.06 percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par World stocks as measured by the MSCI  were up 0.09 percent, just below\par a 3-year high.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par December 10-year JGB futures  slipped 0.25 points to 138.55 in reaction\par to the stock market's climb and on caution ahead of a 1.9 trillion yen\par auction of 10-year JGBs on Thursday.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Standard & Poor's 500 Index  put on 0.08 percent to 1,182.65 and\par the Nasdaq Composite Index         edged 0.03 percent lower to\par 2,101.97.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041129e0bt002xm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
