\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar stuck near lows, central banks a worry.  \b0\par\par\uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2004\par 02:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Eric Burroughs\par  TOKYO, Nov 29 (Reuters) - The dollar hovered near record lows\par  against the euro and a 4-=   year low against the yen on Monday\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 as the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remained vulnerable to worries about major central banks trimming down dollar assets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 But profit-taking on short positions gave a small lift to the dollar, which has shed nearly 9 percent against the euro and yen in two months. Market players were also keeping an eye out for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "A lot of people are nervous and quick to take profits," said one head \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a European bank.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's latest woes came on Friday from a report, later denied, that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut back on U.S. Treasuries in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The report heightened one of the market's biggest fears: that Asian central banks in particular are becoming wary of funding the record U.S. current account deficit and holding dollar investments.  \par \par \uc2 Worries about the United States' ability to fund its current account and budget deficits have pushed the dollar repeatedly lower in recent weeks, while investors have bet Washington prefers a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help resolve its imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is focused squarely on further dollar downside," Singapore-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at UBS Investment Bank said in a note to clients Monday.  \par \par \uc2 In early Tokyo trade, the dollar recovered to $1.3240 against the euro compared with Friday's a record low of $1.3330. The dollar has hit a record low against the euro for four consecutive trading sessions.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen , the dollar traded at 102.90 yen - up from Friday's 102.15 yen, the lowest since March 2000. The dollar had traded all the way back up to 103.45 yen on profit-taking before resuming its downward slide.  \par \par \uc2 The yen remained in a range against the euro , trading at 136.25 yen.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH  \par \par \uc2 Market players are keeping an eye out for sustained profit-taking that could lift the dollar even more, as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister, Sadakazu Tanigaki, repeated on Monday his warning that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements need to be watched closely and that Japan will take decisive action when needed, but traders increasingly view such remarks as hollow.  \par \par \uc2 The UBS analysts noted that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a "big worry", but that the yen's gains have been mostly fuelled by speculative bets rather than investment flows into Japan, potentially limiting any further appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Traders once again looked past economic data. Japanese retail sales fell 1.4 percent in October, much worse than forecast, though the figures were distorted by the effect of typhoons and a deadly earthquake during the month.  \par \par \uc2 Economic data in Japan, Europe and the United States have mattered little for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  however, with the focus instead on the U.S. deficits and the widespread belief the dollar is heading even lower.  \par \par \uc2 Market players were still mulling Friday's report from a Shanghai business newspaper that quoted a policy board member of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as saying it had started cutting its dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which it cited at $180 billion.  \par \par \uc2 But that number was tough to square with available data.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury figures showed Chinese investors holding $174 billion of U.S. Treasuries in September, with the bulk likely held by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 China is believed to have already started diversifying its reserves beyond dollars, especially with its total reserves up 27 percent this year to $514 billion. But 80 percent of that is thought to be in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 While China is a big buyer of Treasuries, it does not come close to Japan. Japan's total holdings of Treasuries were up 23 percent between January and September to $720 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, speculation that China could soon loosen the peg of its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar and let it appreciate has fuelled dollar losses against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao played down the likelihood of a yuan revaluation coming soon, saying over the weekend that China needed a stable economy and healthy financial system to unshackle the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.  \par \par \uc2 Wen also said China was unlikely to make such a move amid intense speculation.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041129e0bt001uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
