\par\par \b \uc2 WEEK AHEAD-Tokyo stocks seen hampered by dollar weakness.  \b0\par\par\uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2004\par 06:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Reuters will replace this item with a new Asia WEEK AHEAD share market preview on December 17 under the code  \par \par \uc2 )  \par \par \uc2 By Edwina Gibbs  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 28 (Reuters) - Tokyo stocks will find it hard to move higher this week, with the dollar's slump against the yen set to hog the spotlight again and keep buying activity in check.  \par \par \uc2 But barring another dollar slide, solid corporate fundamentals - evidenced by banking sector progress in dealing with bad loans, strong auto sales and healthy earnings outlooks for steel and shipping firms - are likely to underpin the market.  \par \par \uc2 A mid-quarter update from technology bellwether Intel Corp (INTC.O) on Tuesday will be watched for signs of further weakness in the chip industry after chip-making stocks like Elpida Memory Inc and NEC Electronics Corp (6723.T) were pummelled last week.  \par \par \uc2 Investors will also be watching the U.S. holiday shopping season for clues to the strength of the world's biggest economy.  \par \par \uc2 "The key is going to be where \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  go," said Takahiko Murai, general manager of equities at Nozomi Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is likely to keep testing levels to see where the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar weakness sent the Nikkei benchmark average 2.25 percent lower last week.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, it dropped 0.6 percent to 10,833.75, as the dollar hit a 4-1/2-year low against the yen on a report that China had pared its U.S. Treasury bond holdings.  \par \par \uc2 That followed a large drop on Monday after U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan warned that markets' appetite for the dollar could dwindle.  \par \par \uc2 A stronger yen cuts the value of Japanese firms' overseas earnings and makes their exports more expensive.  \par \par \uc2 In U.S markets on Friday, the Dow Jones industrial average rose 0.02 percent and the Nasdaq Composite Index dipped 0.03 percent in lacklustre half-day trade after Thanksgiving.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar bounced back a little after the banker quoted in the China business newspaper report issued a statement saying he had no knowledge of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action on reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo market participants said that while dollar weakness was weighing on shares, the yen had not strengthened against the euro and exporters appeared to have hedged several months ahead, limiting stock market downside.  \par \par \uc2 First-half earnings from the banking sector last week have also supported Tokyo stocks. Although profit performance was mixed, most banks have made significant progress in dealing with the bad loans that have dogged them for a decade.  \par \par \uc2 "The electronics sector is not doing well but we have banks showing progress in structural reform and auto makers with strong sales results," said Takashi Kamiya, chief strategist and asset allocator at T & D Asset Management.  \par \par \uc2 "Two out of the three sectors with the biggest weightings in the stock market are doing fine," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Steel firms like Nippon Steel Corp (5401.T) and JFE Holdings Inc (5411.T) have found favour with market again after Nissan Motor Co (7201.T) said it was suffering a shortage of steel as have shipping firms like Nippon Yusen KK. (9101.T) as dry cargo freight rates hit an all-time high on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Preliminary industrial output for October on Tuesday and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's corporate capital spending survey for the July-September quarter on Friday are set to attract attention with investors keeping an eye out for any more signs that the economy's pace of recovery is slowing.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of 26 economists produced a median forecast for a month-on-month rise of 0.1 percent for industrial output, as growth sputtered on weak demand in the electronics sector and typhoons and an earthquake hit production.  \par \par \uc2 Several insurers, including Millea Holdings Inc (8766.T), the nation's biggest non-life insurer, will report first half earnings on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Most have issued profit warnings, saying first-half income would be hit badly by the spate of typhoons but adding that full year results would be little affected as they would dip into special reserves.  \par \par \uc2 DIARY  \par \par \uc2 Monday  \par \par \uc2 - Millea Holdings Inc (8766.T) first-half earnings  \par \par \uc2 Tuesday  \par \par \uc2 - Preliminary industrial output for October  \par \par \uc2 - Unemployment and jobs-to-applicants ratio for October.  \par \par \uc2 - Workers household spending for October  \par \par \uc2 - E*Trade Securities , a unit of Softbank Investment Corp. (8473.T), to list its shares on the Jasdaq market for startups.  \par \par \uc2 Friday  \par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  releases corporate capital spending survey for the July-September quarter.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041128e0bs001up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
