\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Nov 26  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2004\par 10:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong's third-quarter gross domestic product grew 1.9% in the third quarter of 2004, a slowdown from growth of 2.6% in the previous three months, government data issued Friday show. However, the expansion in Hong Kong's exports continued to pick up pace in October. (DATA VIEW to run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA WATCH: Authorities in China continue to make strides in reforming how the country's debt markets operate, and next year they are proposing a number of changes that could move the financial system closer to one that allocates credit based on levels of risk, according to people briefed on the plans. ("=CHINA WATCH: Bond Market Reforms To Continue In 2005," ran at 0552 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore appears to have joined an expanding group of Asian central bankers seeking to curb an excessive rise in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Respect MAS But Sell Into Any USD/SGD Rallies," ran at 0826 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: Improved revenue collection and tigther control on spending kept the Philippines budget deficit well below the ceiling in the 10 months to October, spurring confidence in the government's sincerity toward bringing its fiscal house in order. (UPDATE to run by 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's finance minister issued a fresh warning Friday over big swings in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, saying the government stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to curb such moves if necessary. ("\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tanigaki: To Take Stern \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Action As Needed," ran at 0110 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The New Zealand dollar hit a 16-year high against the U.S. dollar in overnight trade, thanks to a rise in the euro and continued demand for New Zealand dollar-denominated bonds. ("NZ Dollar Hits 16-Year High Vs US$; May Add To Gains" ran at 2023 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =Australia Corp Bonds: Australia's corporate bond market is closing out 2004 in remarkable form, having already demolished previous records for new vanilla issuance and with investor demand showing no signs of slowing. ("=Australia Corp Bonds: Issuance Momentum To Roll In 2005," ran at 0324 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australian Prime Minister John Howard said Friday he wouldn't want the Australian dollar to continue climbing as that would undermine the nation's global competitiveness. ("Australia's PM Says Wouldn't Want A$ To Keep Rising," ran at 2208 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: The Indonesian economy improved in the third quarter, thanks to continued growth in private consumption, a surprise uptick in investment and higher exports. ("DATA SNAP: Indonesia 3Q GDP +5.0% On Year, Investment Up," ran at 0742 GMT; DATA VIEW to run by around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: European investors have taken up three-quarters of Chinese oil company CNOOC's five-year US$850 million convertible bond, showing the confidence of western financial markets in the sustainability of China's galloping economy. (INTERVIEW with one of the deal's bookrunners to run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's current account surplus eased to $2.50 billion in October, from $2.81 billion in September, as imports grew at a quicker pace than exports. But the November surplus is expected to remain at a similar level as last month. ("=UPDATE: S Korea Curr Acct Surplus Eases To $2.50B In Oct," ran at 0631 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Tokyo's core consumer price index fell 0.3% on year in November, the government said Friday, extending the string of on-year declines dating back to October 1999. ("Tokyo Nov Core CPI Down 0.3% On Year;Mkt Expected Dn 0.2%," first take ran at 2338 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The head of Hong Kong's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  predicted that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators who are betting on a revaluation of the Chinese yuan by buying the Hong Kong dollar will eventually see the error of their ways, and take money out of Hong Kong. ("HKMA Chief Yam Predicts Outflow Of Money From HK Dollar," ran at 0207 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: An academic adviser to the People's Bank of China said his comments on the composition of the country's U.S. bond holdings was based on U.S. Federal Reserve information rather than any insider knowlege of the country's foreign reserve management. Reported comments by Yu Yongding had caused a minor stir in international money markets as they were interpreted as a sign that China was selling its U.S. assets. (Story to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The International Monetary Fund believes the South Korean economy is headed for better times in 2005, advising further easing of monetary policy and more aggressive fiscal spending to restore "confidence" in the economic growth potential. ("IMF: Korean Econ To Pick Up '05, Monetary Easing Needed," ran at 0137 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Upper House of Parliament Friday approved the appointment of Credit Suisse First Boston's chief fixed-income strategist in Tokyo, Atsushi Mizuno, to replace Bank of Japan board member Teizo Taya. ("Japan's Upper House Approves Mizuno As BOJ Board Member," ran at 0537 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's industrial output rose a modest 2.4% year-on-year in October, sharply slower than September's 12.5%, according to data from the Economic Development Board Friday. It was the weakest on-year gain since March. ("=DATA VIEW: Singapore Oct Indus Output Up 2.4% Vs Yr Ago," ran at 0828 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =China Money Mkts Week: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept debt traders on their toes this week by pulling cash from the banking system amid fresh worries that interest rates would rise. But the market was swayed mostly by the successful auction of a new domestic benchmark treasury and other factors that showed funds remain ample. ("=China Money Mkts Week: Drain Doesn't Offset Liquidity," ran at 0248 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's insurance firms will be allowed to invest directly in yuan-denominated stocks when they have better risk management capabilities, a senior official at the insurance regulator said Friday. ("China To OK Insurers Stk Invest When Risk Mgmt Is Better," ran at 0453 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Khazanah Nasional Bhd., the investment arm of Malaysia's finance ministry, Friday said it may tap international investors to raise funds, speeding up its plan to be an active strategic investor in the region. ("Malaysia Khazanah May Opt To Tap International Investors," ran at 0141 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's six major life insurers Friday reported higher profits for the first half of the fiscal year and a stronger financial position, but said they have failed so far to stem policy cancellations. (UPDATE to run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Training a blowtorch on Australia's red-hot mobiles market, Hutchison Whampoa's local unit has wheeled out a new set of call plans in time for Christmas. ("=Hutchison Australia Rachets Up Mobiles Competition," ran at 0432 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australian nickel miner WMC Resources Ltd. Friday urged shareholders to ignore all correspondence from hostile bidder Xstrata PLC. ("=UPDATE: WMC Resources Says Ignore Xstrata, Bid Too Low," ran at 0437 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Kanebo Ltd., which is restructuring under the wing of a government-backed rehabilitation body, said Friday it wiped out its capital deficit by the Sept. 30 end of the first fiscal half. ("Japan's Kanebo 1H Group Net Profit Y358.1B," first take ran at 0241 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: The Government of Singapore Investment Corp. is buying a major stake in a Malaysian resort company, adding to a recent spate of investments in the city-state's northern neighbor by government-linked companies. (Story will run by around 0920 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Liquid crystal display panel makers such as Samung Electronics Co. and LG.Philps LCD Co. may have to delay full-capacity ramp up of their next-generation plants due to problems with some optical equipment supplied by Japan's Canon Inc., analysts say. But this could be a boon for the LCD market, as it could limit the supply of panels coming onstream in 2005, and help stabilize falling prices. (Story to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: David Eldon, chairman of Hongkong & Shanghai Banking Corp., the Asian unit of HSBC Holdings PLC (HBC), said Friday it was unlikely the London-based financial services group would contemplate further strategic investments in Chinese banks as it is already "stretched thin" by its existing tie ups. (Story to run by 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Hong Kong-listed Greencool Technology Holdings Ltd. said Wednesday it acquired a French autoparts maker and a British auto design firm as part of its effort to enhance its auto making technology. (Story to run by around 1045 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =Asian Stock Focus: Shares of Japan's Furukawa Electric Co., the world's largest maker of optical-fiber networks by production volume, have surged recently on speculation that it is in line to get a slew of new orders.("Asian Stock Focus: Furukawa Elec Shrs Leap On Order Hopes," ran at 0030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: SembCorp Industries Ltd., a Singapore government-linked conglomerate, Friday said it will raise provisions by S$200 million and warned there may be more after losing a legal battle in the U.K. against Swiss Allseas Group SA over a botched ship conversion contract. ("=UPDATE: SembCorp Ups Allseas Suit Provisions By S$200M," ran at 0605 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Seiko Epson Corp. said Friday it and Toyo Communication Equipment Co. have agreed to merge their quartz device \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October next year. ("Seiko Epson, Toyo Commun To Merge Crystal Ops Oct 2005," first take ran at 0646 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: NTT DoCoMo Inc. expects a new discount service to reduce its revenue by up to Y50 billion in the next fiscal year through March 2006, NTT DoCoMo President Masao Nakamura said Friday. (Story to run shortly) [ 26-11-04 0928GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Daikyo Inc., which is restructuring under the guidance of a government-backed rehabilitation body, said Friday it swung deep into the red for the first half ended Sept. 30, due to restructuring steps and losses from the adoption of a stricter accounting method. ("Japan's Daikyo 1H Group Net Loss Y251.67B Vs Y3.19B Pft," first take ran at 0848 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Hyundai Motor Co. workers are staging a partial walkout Friday to support a nationwide strike organized by the Korean Confederation of Trade Unions, or KCTU. ("S Korea Hyundai Motor Workers Staging Partial Walkout Fri," ran at 0326 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Suntec City Development Ltd.'s S$722 million initial public offering of its real estate investment trust will be priced almost a week earlier owing to strong demand for high-yielding assets, market sources said Friday. ("=Singapore Suntec To Price Earlier As Demand Good-Sources," ran at 0456 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Woolworths Ltd., Australia's second largest retailer, is evaluating China and India as markets that could provide growth in the longer term, chairman James Strong said Friday. ("=UPDATE: Australia's Woolworths May Seek Asian Growth," ran at 0540 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: BHP Billiton Ltd. is exploring options to return a further US$220 million to shareholders following the completion of its off-market buyback this week. ("BHP Billiton Mulls Options For US$220 Mln Capital Return," ran at 0019 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The Warehouse Group Ltd. said Friday that it will put more emphasis on "quality" at its core Red Sheds discount stores in a bid to turn around weak sales. ("=2nd UPDATE: NZ Warehouse's CEO To Focus On "Quality"," ran at 0331 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Shineway Pharmaceutical Group Ltd., which is planning a listing on the Hong Kong stock exchange in December, expects net profit in 2004 to grow at least 65%, boosted by robust demand for traditional Chinese medicine. ("=INTERVIEW: China Shineway Expects 2004 Net To Jump 65%," ran at 0051 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: The Philippines will hike the import duty on crude and refined oil products, except liquefied petroleum gas, in January to raise around PHP5.58 billion in additional revenue to help fill a yawning budget gap, the Department of Energy said Friday. ("Philippines To Raise Oil Import Tax To 5% In Jan From 3%," first take ran at 0639 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN POWER: Japan's auto makers, retailers, and shopping mall owners are spurning the country's incumbent power utilities and making their own electricity. ("=JAPAN POWER: Auto Makers, Retailers Go their Own Way," ran at 0618 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan, South Korea, China and the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, collectively known as ASEAN+3, plan to hold a high- level meeting in spring next year to discuss the next steps in building the region's strategic oil reserves. ("Asean+3 To Hold Talks Early '05 On Strategic Oil Reserves," ran at 0448 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Tohoku Electric Power Co. has received its first Russian crude oil cargo and may import more Russian crude to offset the loss of supplies from China's Daqing field. ("Tohoku Elec Eyes Russian Crude As Supply From China Drops," ran at 0302 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA CRUDE OUTLOOK: Asian oil refiners to begin picking up spot cargoes for January next week; light grades, both sour and sweet, are facing their first discounts in months. (Story to run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA PRODUCTS OUTLOOK: Naphtha and fuel oil prices should remain supported in coming days by strong demand, while South America arbitrage possibilities may underpin a gasoil market weighed down by rising supplies in South Korea and Singapore. ("=ASIA PRODUCTS OUTLOOK: Naphtha, HSFO Firm; Gasoil Arb," ran at 0846 GMT) [ 26-11-04 0928GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041126e0bq000fg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
