\par\par \b \uc2 AWSJ(11/26) Japan Officials Stay On Sidelines As Yen Surges  \b0\par\par\uc2 863 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2004\par 05:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 AWSJ(11/26) Japan Officials Stay On Sidelines As Yen Surges  \par \par \uc2 (From THE ASIAN WALL STREET JOURNAL)  \par \par \uc2 By Sebastian Moffett and Jathon Sapsford  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo -- AS THE YEN HAS SOARED to more than four-year highs against the dollar this past couple of weeks, Japan has so far stuck to a surprising new strategy on exchange rates: do nothing and watch the yen rise.  \par \par \uc2 While no one expects the inaction to last, many analysts are betting that Japanese financial authorities may let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise still further than the levels it traded at Thursday, when it reached a 4 1/2-year high of 102.41 yen per dollar. Previously, Japan had fought to defend a level of 105 yen to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The yen has strengthened over the past month to its current level from 109 to the dollar. Japan takes this seriously because it depends so much on exports for growth. A rising yen can cut into Japanese exports by making them more expensive in terms of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The stronger yen also diminishes the value of earnings in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they are converted into yen. Sony Corp. says it earns roughly 33 billion yen ($321 million) less each year for every yen the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciates against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has a reputation for jumping in to try to either stop or slow the yen's rise. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted a record 20 trillion yen of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2003, followed by a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 But during the current round of yen strength, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  once, even though the latest gains have taken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to higher levels than triggered past \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 One possible reason is that it would be hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, because there is increasing international pressure for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be allowed to float -- in particular, on the yuan, which is pegged against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Another consideration is whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be effective. 'The effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  [to strengthen the dollar] would only be short-lived and limited,' Kaoru Yosano, policy chief of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party, said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 So far, officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- which decides \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy -- and people close to the ministry have merely said it's time to think about restarting such a policy. On Thursday, former Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda said he saw no need for the dollar to go lower than it already has.  \par \par \uc2 'I don't think the U.S. dollar is overvalued in terms of its effective exchange rate,' Mr. Kuroda told a financial forum. 'At the moment, I see no need for the dollar to depreciate.'  \par \par \uc2 But analysts say the biggest reason for the hands-off approach so far is that corporate Japan can take it.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's tankan surveys of the business environment showed firms worked on the assumption of an exchange rate of 114 yen to the dollar in the year to March 31, 2004, but are assuming 106 for the current fiscal year. To deal with this, Japanese companies have ramped up their decade-long campaign to move production offshore -- and procure more parts for that production overseas -- and thus become less vulnerable to exchange-rate fluctuations. Since the late 1990s, Mazda Motor Corp. has been importing engines from its U.S. affiliate, Ford Motor Co., which it then puts in cars it produces in Japan. And to reduce exposure on the products they do export, Japanese auto makers employ sophisticated hedging techniques.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, Japanese companies are fitter as a whole, so they can better absorb external shocks. Toyota Motor Corp., for example, has roughly $30 billion in reserves. Following the yen's rise, Goldman Sachs Group on Wednesday lowered its forecast for Toyota's consolidated operating profit for the fiscal year ending March 31, 2005, by just 4.1%, to 1.7 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 According to forecasts released with interim earnings reports for the six months to Sept. 30, publicly listed Japanese companies expect profits in the fiscal year to March 31, 2005, to grow 21% from the previous year -- itself a record year.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say this helps explain the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reluctance to act at current exchange-rate levels even though economic growth is going through a slow patch; the government is expected to report a small contraction for the July-September quarter when data are released Dec. 8. In addition to healthy corporate profits, banks are clearing up their bad loans -- the scourge of Japan's economy throughout the past decade -- and prices seem to be stabilizing. The government appears to have abandoned its old fears that the economy might plunge back into semi-permanent recession, and can now tolerate the yen strengthening a little past 105 per dollar, says Tomoko Fujii, an economist at Nikko Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 'The [\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 105 yen because the Japanese economy recovered,' says Ms. Fujii. Still, she says, 'Everyone expects \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the rate tests 100.'  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 Phred Dvorak contributed to this article.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041126e0bq002p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
