\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips as market ponders China reserve plans.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrea Ricci  \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2004\par 20:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 26 (Reuters) - The dollar weakened on Friday but hovered above record lows against the euro plumbed overnight on comments, later retracted, that China was cutting back on its accumulation of dollar assets.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slid to a record low of $1.3329 per euro during overnight trading in Asia after a Chinese business newspaper quoted a member of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaking committee as saying that China had already begun reducing U.S. dollar assets in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 "In the state that the market is in right now, where there is a lot of speculation about reserve shifts, the comments from China were taken quite seriously," said Bob Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York.  \par \par \uc2 It was the fourth straight day that the dollar has set a fresh record low against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar bounced back after the banker quoted in the article issued a statement saying he had, in fact, no knowledge of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action on reserves. But traders quickly jumped in to sell the dollar on rallies, as has been the pattern in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 By midafternoon in New York, the euro was up 0.38 percent against the dollar at $1.3292.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the market showed little reaction to comments later on Friday by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Jean-Claude Trichet, who was in Rio de Janeiro.  \par \par \uc2 Trichet said that recent moves in the euro/dollar were "unwelcome," and that it underlined the importance of the U.S. Treasury pursuing its strong dollar policy.  \par \par \uc2 "The market was looking for something to change the perceived threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the ECB, and he hasn't said anything in that direction," said Lynch.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said trade was thin and choppy with other U.S. markets closing early after the Thanksgiving holiday.  \par \par \uc2 SENSITIVE ABOUT CENTRAL BANKS  \par \par \uc2 For a number of years, the gaping United States current account deficit has been partly financed by purchases of Treasuries by foreign central banks. China is the second-biggest holder of U.S. assets after Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Weekly U.S. custody holdings data show that foreign central banks have continued to buy more Treasuries recently, but at a slower pace than during the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this year.  \par \par \uc2 So far this year, foreign central banks have bought more than $200 billion of Treasuries and own nearly 30 percent of publicly held U.S. Treasury debt.  \par \par \uc2 "We have seen no evidence in the market yet that a large shift in Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings has occurred out of U.S. dollars and into euros. It would be very surprising for this to have occurred without the market's knowledge," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York.  \par \par \uc2 Still, with most analysts expecting central banks to diversify out of dollars over time, even a hint that China might be backing off from Treasuries was enough to roil the market, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "When one of your most reliable, solid buyers (China) is cutting back ... one of the horses has bolted," said Richard Franulovich, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Westpac Banking Corp. in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a 4-1/2-year low of 102.18 yen according to Reuters data, but was trading at 102.50 in the U.S. afternoon, nearly flat on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The pound hit a nine-month high of $1.9040 in Asia, within sight of 12-year highs and was trading at $1.8964 in New York, up 0.39 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also recovered from nine-year lows against an index of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Swiss franc, 12-year lows against the Canadian dollar and 16-year lows against the New Zealand dollar set in Asia.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041126e0bq00dqk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
