\par\par \b \uc2 ECONOMY WEEKAHEAD-U.S. Payrolls,  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  take centre stage.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe  \par \uc2 732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2004\par 14:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 26 (Reuters) - U.S. payrolls numbers are sure to take centre stage next week as markets seek confirmation of a pick up in job creation, while a frail dollar means \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  developments should remain a key focus.  \par \par \uc2 With the dollar weak against the Japanese yen and euro, analysts say the spectre of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese and euro zone authorities has grown. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets on Thursday and attention is on what it will say about the euro's export-damaging rise against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The week ahead will be the busiest of December, with a raft of U.S. and European economic numbers on the calendar.  \par \par \uc2 The November U.S. non-farm payrolls report is due for release on Friday and economists polled by Reuters forecast a 180,000 gain compared to a robust 337,000 increase in October.  \par \par \uc2 "The market expectation of a number close to 200,000 is reasonable," said America Express Bank economist Kevin Grice.  \par \par \uc2 "There is some speculation that the risk is to the upside as the October number didn't fully capture people returning to work after the hurricanes."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said a second month of strong job gains would make a quarter point rate hike in December a done deal.  \par \par \uc2 Data during the week is likely to be scrutinised for clues to the monthly jobs report. The Institute for Supply Management releases its manufacturing survey on Wednesday, while its non-manufacturing report is out on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The preliminary reading of U.S. GDP in the third quarter is due on Tuesday. November consumer confidence data and the Federal Reserve's Beige Book survey of economic conditions will also be released during the week.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  TALK  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are in focus as traders assess whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) or European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem a rise in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar. The euro hit a record high on Friday above $1.33 . A firm \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes exports more expensive and hurts economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 Reuters' discussions with euro zone monetary officials over the past week have shown that the ECB is watching the euro's climb closely.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is in the mood to push the dollar to the pressure points of both the ECB and BOJ. There is a very strong chance that we could see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week, with the BOJ moving first," said Grice at American Express Bank.  \par \par \uc2 It is against this backdrop that the ECB holds its monetary policy meeting on Thursday. It is widely tipped to leave interest rates steady at 2.0 percent and with the euro strong, markets are betting rates will stay low for some to come.  \par \par \uc2 "Attention will be on the press conference as markets try to gauge the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the potential for interest rate cuts," said James Knightley, economist at ING Financial in London.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone data are likely to be scrutinised for signs that the economic outlook has taken a turn for the worse. The European Commission's sentiment survey is out on Tuesday, followed on Wednesday by euro zone purchasing managers' surveys for the manufacturing sector and on Friday by service sector surveys.  \par \par \uc2 Tuesday sees the release of the flash estimate of the euro zone consumer price index. News this week that Germany's annual inflation rate slowed in November bodes well for the euro zone figure, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 "The preliminary German harmonised inflation numbers for November showed a big fall in the annual inflation rate from 2.2 percent to 1.8 percent," said economists at HSBC in a note.  \par \par \uc2 They expect euro zone inflation to slow to 2.1 percent from October's rate of 2.4 percent.  \par \par \uc2 In Britain, the focus is on the testimony of Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee members to a parliamentary committee on Tuesday and the government's pre-budget report on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the MPC testimony would be more important to the market amid growing expectations that UK rates have peaked and that the next BOE move could be a rate cut.  \par \par \uc2 "The BOE is likely to get questions about the housing market, low inflation and the possibility of rate cuts," said ING's Knightley.  \par \par \uc2 The Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) releases its semi-annual Economic Outlook on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041126e0bq009w1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
