\par\par \b \uc2 Japan slows U.S. bond buying, focuses on reserves.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2004\par 11:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 26 (Reuters) - Japan has largely finished buying U.S. Treasuries for its official reserves, barring a resumption of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a factor that could reduce downward pressure on U.S. bond yields.  \par \par \uc2 Even after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-March, it kept buying Treasuries to rebalance its portfolio, which had become heavily weighted in deposits due to the size of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's strength.  \par \par \uc2 But such purchases are now largely over, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official says.  \par \par \uc2 "Unless there is more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there should not be a big change in the current picture," the official told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't have a target ratio for deposits and securities holdings," he added.  \par \par \uc2 While the market is rife with speculation about when Japan might step back into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to halt the rekindled slide in the dollar, which hit its lowest against the yen in nearly five years on Friday, a lack of such action meant the Treasuries market will be lacking a major player.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has bought over $300 billion in foreign securities - believed to be mostly U.S. Treasuries - since January 2003, when Tokyo started massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, selling yen to help its export-reliant economy.  \par \par \uc2 Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign suddenly ended in mid-March, government data shows that Japan kept buying foreign securities until August.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF, which manages Japan's world-record $838 billion foreign reserves, of which $696 billion is in securities and $121 billion in cash deposits, often parks dollars acquired from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in bank deposits before investing them in U.S. securities, so as to avoid a huge impact on the bond market.  \par \par \uc2 "We are a big player so we do not want to cause a disruptive impact in the U.S. bond market," the MOF official said.  \par \par \uc2 The shift to securities from cash deposits was apparent until August, with an average monthly rise of $15 billion in securities holdings since April.  \par \par \uc2 But the pace has since slowed, increasing only $1.9 billion in September and $7.8 billion in October.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said it might take a while for the absence of Japan to have a major impact on the Treasuries market.  \par \par \uc2 "Not very many people in the market are aware of this," said Susumu Kato, chief fixed-income strategist Lehman Brothers.  \par \par \uc2 "What American investors are concerned about at the moment seems to be whether foreign central banks are making an exodus from the U.S. Treasury market."  \par \par \uc2 MANAGING MASSIVE RESERVES  \par \par \uc2 Still, as Japan's external reserves have grown 1.8 times over the last two years, there is growing pressure on the government to better manage its portfolio.  \par \par \uc2 "We want to ensure liquidity of our assets while making utmost efforts to maximise profits. We've been more aggressive than usual in improving our portfolio management," said the MOF official.  \par \par \uc2 Takatoshi Ito, a University of Tokyo professor, said the MOF should increase returns on the reserves by using private-sector fund managers, given that taxpayers could end up footing the bill for the cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , now financed by issuance of finance bills (FBs).  \par \par \uc2 Only about five officials at the MOF are involved on a day-to-day basis in managing the portfolio, whose size is equivalent to 20 percent of the country's GDP, while the Bank of Japan acts as a "back office" to help administrative work.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF official said the ministry was considering diversifying portfolio management, including using private-sector firms as agents to bid in U.S. Treasury auctions, rather than depending solely on the U.S. Federal Reserve.  \par \par \uc2 But he stressed that Japan had no plan to diversify out of dollars into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as euros, since it could precipitate a further decline in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "There is absolutely no such plan," he said. "Such a move would mean a reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for us," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's steady fall to record lows against the euro has fuelled talk that central banks around the world could shift their reserves into the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said earlier this week that it might review the share of euros in its reserves. There is persistent talk that Chinese authorities are thinking the same.  \par \par \uc2 But the MOF official said the share of euros in Japan's reserves would increase only if Japan buys euros for yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 No breakdown of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Japan's foreign reserves is made public, but they are believed to be predominantly dollars and euros after sales of Indonesian rupiah bought in 1997 during Asian financial crisis.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041126e0bq00811\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
