\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Asia gold holds near 16-yr high as dollar slumps.  \b0\par\par\uc2 504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2004\par 04:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 26 (Reuters) - Gold was bullish near a 16-1/4-year high in Asia on Friday as the weak dollar, which hit another record low against the euro earlier in the day, induced solid bids from fund operators, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Sentiment for gold turned increasingly brighter after breaking through closely watched resistance at $450 per ounce on Thursday, but traders were careful about chasing the metal too strongly amid an absence of U.S. players due to the Thanksgiving holiday.  \par \par \uc2 "Basically, traders want to keep their long positions rather than going short before they go home in this atmosphere," said Kaname Gokon, research section manager at Okato Shoji Co. Ltd.  \par \par \uc2 "Although long positions are mounting, many believe that gold has more room to rise."  \par \par \uc2 At 0306 GMT, spot gold was quoted at $451.75/2.50 per ounce, compared with a European close of $451.50/2.25 on Thursday. COMEX and NYMEX markets were closed on Thursday and will be closed on Friday for Thanksgiving.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, bullion rose as high as $452.75, a level last seen in June 1988.  \par \par \uc2 Gold is expected to attract investor interest as long as the dollar stays bearish.  \par \par \uc2 The euro hit a record high of $1.3283 in early Asian trade and the dollar dropped to its lowest in nearly five years against the yen  \par \par \uc2 Metal traders were watching whether central banks would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the dollar's further decline.  \par \par \uc2 "Even if central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I don't think they can completely reverse the trend of a bearish dollar," said a manager at a Japanese trading house.  \par \par \uc2 "There are plenty of investors willing to buy gold when it dips to around $440 should the dollar regain strength after a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but many believe that the effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't last long."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki reiterated on Friday the country's authorities would take action as needed in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if exchange rates moved too rapidly, but his comment had little impact.  \par \par \uc2 The market was looking forward to comments later in the day from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, who is due to speak in Rio de Janeiro, along with ECB council member and Spanish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Jaime Caruana.  \par \par \uc2 Trichet, who referred to recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements as "brutal" earlier in the month, has become the most vocal European policymaker on dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 In the Tokyo futures market, the benchmark October gold futures contract rose in line with spot bullion.  \par \par \uc2 The October contract closed the morning session up 8 yen at 1,494 yen per gram. It had moved in a range of 1,490 to 1,495.  \par \par \uc2 Silver benefitted from gold's firmness. It was at $7.69/$7.72 an ounce from $7.64/$7.67 in Europe on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Platinum was quoted at $863/$868 an ounce, compared with $861/$865.  \par \par \uc2 Its sister metal palladium was little changed at $213/$218.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041126e0bq002ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
