\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls prey to bears, hits life low vs euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2004\par 16:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 25 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a record low against the euro for a third consecutive day on Thursday and hit multi-year lows versus other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as broad dollar selling gathered momentum.  \par \par \uc2 The most eye-catching decline was against the New Zealand dollar, where it touched its lowest point in 16 years. However, trading was thin with U.S. markets closed for Thanksgiving.  \par \par \uc2 Warnings from Japanese and European policymakers did little to halt the greenback's slide, which has been driven primarily by concerns over the U.S. current account deficit and the market's belief that Washington is happy to see a weaker dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "The market has a very well defined trend and it will continue to exploit it until there is official opposition," said Tim Fox, market strategist at National Australia Bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 "But I don't think we are at the point of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet, we may have to break above $1.35 dramatically. And I don't think this is coming in the next week."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar fell to a record low of $1.3237 per euro  but rose back to\par $1.3220 by 1530 GMT. Against the yen, the dollar fell as low as 102.41\par       before recovering a touch to 102.60 yen.\par It notched up new nine-year lows against the Swiss franc  and a basket\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2        .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weaker-than-expected data from Germany, Europe's largest economy, had little effect on the euro. The Ifo research institute's pan-German business climate index fell to 94.1 in November, its lowest since September 2003 and below forecasts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Data from the eurozone is largely being ignored. Everyone is running with the trend," said Kristjan Kasikov, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Calyon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TEASING BANKERS  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many analysts said the dollar's slide could only be stopped by central banks now, and investors were likely to push the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as low as they could to see at what point policymakers would lose their poise and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's reaction was particularly key.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The ECB has to be quite blunt," said Hans Redeker, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BNP Paribas. "Otherwise the markets will force it to see where its threshold is."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ECB will get a chance to comment if it wants later in the session when chief economist Otmar Issing makes a speech. The Bank of England's chief economist Charlie Bean is also due to speak.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of the German Ifo economic institute, Hans-Werner Sinn, said he believed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets was "appropriate" to rein in the dollar's fall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. policymakers have so far appeared happy with a weaker dollar and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decline accelerated last week after Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan said U.S. deficits were unsustainable and appetite for U.S. assets was bound to dwindle.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Japan, officials worried a rising yen would hurt a recovery in the export-oriented economy also stepped up their rhetoric.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Policy Board member Hidehiko Haru said he would pay attention to any negative impact the recent rise in the yen had on the economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's top government spokesman Hiroyuki Hosoda said the yen's recent surge did not reflect fundamentals and that authorities would act against rapid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets since March, after a record 20 trillion yen ($194.4 billion) of dollar-supporting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2003.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IS CHINA READY?  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also pressuring the dollar against the yen was speculation that China may decide to revalue the yuan in the next few days.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The talk mounted on Thursday with the premium on the yuan in the offshore market surging to its highest in more than a year with some analysts saying Beijing could reach a decision at an annual high-level economic meeting this weekend.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is widely believed that freeing the Chinese yuan from its peg of around 8.28 per dollar will result in an appreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and lift other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with it.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has come under heavy pressure from the United States to allow the yuan to appreciate, as some U.S. manufacturers say an artificially cheap yuan hurts jobs and exports.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041125e0bp008qg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\p