\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Bears attack dollar again, euro sets record high.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2004\par 14:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 25 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a record low against the euro for a third consecutive day on Thursday and hit multi-year lows versus all other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as broad dollar selling gathered momentum.  \par \par \uc2 The most eye-catching decline was against the New Zealand dollar, where it touched its lowest point in 16 years.  \par \par \uc2 Warnings from Japanese and European policymakers did little to halt the greenback's slide, which has been driven primarily by concerns over the U.S. current account deficit and the market's belief that Washington is happy to see a weaker dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is on the move," said Hans-Guenter Redeker, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London. "We have again broken significant levels, including $1.32. And if we are able to close above that, we will see more acceleration to $1.3350."  \par \par \uc2 "What we have to think about is what can stop this and when."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a record low of $1.3237 per euro by 1255 GMT. Against the yen, the dollar fell as low as 102.41 before recovering a touch to 102.60 yen.  \par \par \uc2 It notched up new nine-year lows against the Swiss franc and a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Weaker-than-expected data from Europe's largest economy had little effect on the euro. The Ifo research institute's pan-German business climate index fell to 94.1 in November, its lowest since September 2003 and below forecasts.  \par \par \uc2 "Data from the eurozone is largely being ignored. Everyone is running with the trend," said Kristjan Kasikov, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Calyon.  \par \par \uc2 Trading was thin with U.S. markets closed for the Thanksgiving holiday.  \par \par \uc2 CHICKEN  \par \par \uc2 Several investment banks cut their dollar forecasts, with JP Morgan targeting the dollar at 100 yen and the euro at $1.35 by the end of this year.  \par \par \uc2 And analysts said markets were now likely to push the dollar as low as they could to see at what point central banks, in particular the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would lose their poise and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the slide.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. policymakers have so far appeared happy with a weaker dollar and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline accelerated last week after Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan said U.S. deficits were unsustainable and appetite for U.S. assets was bound to dwindle.  \par \par \uc2 "The ECB has to be quite blunt," said Redeker. "Otherwise the markets will force it to see where its threshold is."  \par \par \uc2 The ECB will get a chance to comment if it wants later in the session when chief economist Otmar Issing makes a speech. The Bank of England's chief economist Charlie Bean is also due speak.  \par \par \uc2 The head of the German Ifo economic institute said he believed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was "appropriate" to rein in the dollar's fall.  \par \par \uc2 In Japan, officials worried a rising yen would hurt a recovery in the export-oriented economy also stepped up their rhetoric.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said that recent movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were not stable.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier BOJ Policy Board member Hidehiko Haru said he would pay attention to any negative impact the recent rise in the yen had on the economy.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's top government spokesman Hiroyuki Hosoda said the yen's recent surge did not reflect fundamentals and that authorities would act against rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.  \par \par \uc2 "The overriding sentiment on the dollar is very, very negative and it's a question of playing chicken with the BOJ as to whether they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  or not," said Tony Norfield, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at ABN AMRO in London.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since March, after a record 20 trillion yen ($194.4 billion) of dollar-supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 IS CHINA READY?  \par \par \uc2 Also pressuring the dollar against the yen was speculation that China may decide to revalue the yuan in the next few days.  \par \par \uc2 The talk mounted on Thursday with the premium on the yuan in the offshore market surging to its highest in more than a year with some analysts saying Beijing could reach a decision at an annual high-level economic meeting this weekend.  \par \par \uc2 It is widely believed that freeing the Chinese yuan from its peg of around 8.28 per dollar will result in an appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lift other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with it.  \par \par \uc2 China has come under heavy pressure from the United States to allow the yuan to appreciate, as some U.S. manufacturers say an artificially cheap yuan hurts jobs and exports.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041125e0bp007px\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
