\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar sinks to record low for third day.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Hunt  \par \uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2004\par 12:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 25 (Reuters) - The dollar sank to an all-time low versus the euro for a third consecutive day on Thursday and fell to a 4-1/2 year trough on the yen, hit by concerns about U.S. deficits and the view Washington is happy to see a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Warnings from Japanese and European policymakers did little to halt the dollar's slide, which accelerated last week after Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan said U.S. deficits were unsustainable and appetite for U.S. assets was bound to dwindle.  \par \par \uc2 "We have moved into a new stage of the dollar decline and (the dollar's fall) has become one of the policy tools policymakers use. The move is very much a full-fledged policy event. Until policymakers truly protest, what's going to stop the trend?," said Jim McCormick, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar  fell to a record low of $1.3235 per euro by 1130 GMT.\par Against the yen       , the dollar fell as low as 102.41 before\par recovering a touch to 102.54 yen. It has lost more than eight percent\par against the yen since late September.\par The dollar also set new lows against other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - a nine-year low\par against the Swiss franc , a 16-year trough on the New Zealand dollar\par and a nine-year low against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2        .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weaker-than-expected data from Europe's largest economy had little effect on the euro. The Ifo research institute's pan-German business climate index fell to 94.1 in November, its lowest since September 2003 and below forecasts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Data from the eurozone is largely being ignored. Everyone is running with the trend," said Kristjan Kasikov, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Calyon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SNOWBALLING  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Several investment banks cut their dollar forecasts, with JP Morgan targeting the dollar at 100 yen and the euro at $1.35 by the end of this year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French Finance Minister Nicolas Sarkozy said that the United States must be determined to reduce its deficits so that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does not distort trade exchanges.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese officials, worried a rising yen would hurt a recovery in the export-oriented economy, also stepped up their rhetoric.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said that recent movements in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market were not stable.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier BOJ Policy Board member Hidehiko Haru said he would pay attention to any negative impact the recent rise in the yen had on the economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's top government spokesman Hiroyuki Hosoda said the yen's recent surge did not reflect fundamentals and that authorities would act against rapid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The overiding sentiment on dollar is very, very negative and it's a question of playing chicken with the BOJ as to whether they \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or not," said Tony Norfield, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at ABN AMRO.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets since March, after a record 20 trillion yen ($194.4 billion) of dollar-supporting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2003.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 YUAN TALK  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also pressuring the dollar against the yen was speculation that China may decide to revalue the yuan in the next few days.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The talk mounted on Thursday with the premium on the yuan in the offshore market surging to its highest in more than a year with some analysts saying Beijing could reach a decision at an annual high-level economic meeting this weekend.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is widely believed that freeing the Chinese yuan from its peg of around 8.28 per dollar will result in an appreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and lift other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with it.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has come under heavy pressure from the United States to allow the yuan to appreciate, as some U.S. manufacturers say an artificially cheap yuan hurts jobs and exports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Later on Thursday, the chief economists of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Bank of England are expected to make speeches in separate appearances.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041125e0bp006px\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
