\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar stalls, in sight of record low vs euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2004\par 08:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 25 (Reuters) - The dollar stayed in sight of record lows against the euro on Thursday, as investors took a break from pummeling the dollar ahead of a round of economic data from Europe.  \par \par \uc2 With U.S. markets closed for the Thanksgiving holiday, traders said they were waiting on European economic data to decide whether to beat the dollar down past the record low of $1.3190 per euro hit just before the start of Tokyo trade.  \par \par \uc2 A survey from Germany's IFO research institute, due at 0900 GMT, is expected to show that business sentiment in the euro zone's biggest economy deteriorated slightly in November, but dealers said the data was unlikely to cut short the euro's rise.  \par \par \uc2 "Any rallies in the dollar will be limited because there are still quite a lot of people looking to buy euros and sell dollars," said Luke Waddington, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "Even a bad IFO would just be looked on as a chance to buy the euro on dips."  \par \par \uc2 The euro has gained around eight percent against the dollar since the beginning of September, as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under pressure from a view that a weaker dollar was needed to help cut the gaping U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 At 0644 GMT, the dollar was at around $1.3180 per euro compared with $1.3186 in late New York trade.  \par \par \uc2 It fetched around 102.80 yen, barely changed on the day after grinding down to a four-and-a-half year low of 102.56 yen in overseas trade.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said one factor keeping the yen in check was an apparent strengthening of the language used by Japanese officials in their warnings on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.  \par \par \uc2 Chief Cabinet Secretary Hiroyuki Hosoda said on Thursday it was clear that recent yen strength did not reflect fundamentals and that the authorities would act against rapid moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 "There's an acknowledgement that they (the Japanese authorities) are going to step in at some point, but the market is going to keep testing the authorities until it actually happens," said Yoshi Yanagisawa, treasury manager at State Street Bank.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since March, after a record 20 trillion yen ($194.4 billion) of dollar-supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 YUAN SPECULATION  \par \par \uc2 Traders also focused on as Asian Wall Street Journal report in which Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Zhou Xiaochuan hinted that a further slackening of Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls could be on the way.  \par \par \uc2 However, the market stopped short of selling dollars on the report due to reassurances from Zhou that any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform would be gradual.  \par \par \uc2 "They (Chinese authorities) have been throwing a lot of curve balls, offering a little information here and there. But they're saying they will ease gradually, so it's not going to be a big shocker when it happens," said a trader at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 It is widely believed that freeing the Chinese yuan from its peg of around 8.28 per dollar will result in an appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lift other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with it.  \par \par \uc2 China has come under heavy pressure from the United States to allow the yuan to appreciate, as some manufacturers say an artificially cheap yuan hurts jobs and exports.  \par \par \uc2 The market showed little reaction to Japanese data released in the morning, which showed that the trade surplus in October rose 8.8 percent from a year earlier, better than forecasts for a rise of 2.8 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Exports rose to a record high, but the pace of the increase slowed from earlier this year as demand from the United States and China tapered off.  \par \par \uc2 The IFO index of the German business climate is expected to show a fall to 94.7 in November from 95.3 the previous month.  \par \par \uc2 The chief economists of the ECB and the Bank of England are also expected to make speeches in separate appearances. ($1=102.85 yen) (Additional reporting by Naomi Tajitsu).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041125e0bp004y9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
