\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar edges up vs euro, but record low in sight.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu  \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2004\par 07:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 25 (Reuters) - The dollar inched up but stayed in sight of record lows against the euro on Thursday, as investors took a break from pummeling the dollar ahead of a round of economic data from Europe.  \par \par \uc2 With U.S. markets closed for the Thanksgiving holiday, traders said they were waiting on European economic data to decide whether to beat the dollar down past the record low of $1.3190 per euro hit just before the start of Tokyo trade.  \par \par \uc2 "It's hard to say if we'll be seeing any major moves today due to the holiday, but with some key data coming out, there will be plenty of opportunity for the dollar to fluctuate," said a strategist at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, some analysts said that concerns about yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had calmed for the moment, given the apparent hesitation of the Japanese authorities to stray from standard rhetoric, despite the dollar's slide against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Chief Cabinet Secretary Hiroyuki Hosoda said that recent yen strength did not reflect fundamentals and that the authorities would act against rapid moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 Some in the market said that such comments lacked conviction, and they see a fall in the dollar to 100 yen by the end of December as a real possibility.  \par \par \uc2 The last time the dollar was at that level was in November 1995.  \par \par \uc2 "There's an acknowledgement that they're going to step in at some point, but the market is going to keep testing the authorities until it actually happens," said Yoshi Yanagisawa, treasury manager at State Street Bank.  \par \par \uc2 At 0316 GMT, the dollar was at around $1.3175 per euro , compared with $1.3186 in late New York trade.  \par \par \uc2 It was at around 102.85 yen after grinding down to a four-and-a-half year low of 102.56 yen in overseas trade.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also focused on a newspaper report in which Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Zhou Xiaochuan hinted that a further slackening of Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls could be on the way.  \par \par \uc2 However, the market stopped short of selling dollars on the report due to reassurances from Zhou that any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform would be gradual.  \par \par \uc2 "They (Chinese authorities) have been throwing a lot of curve balls, offering a little information here and there. But they're saying they will ease gradually, so it's not going to be a big shocker when it happens," said a trader at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 "The factor of orderliness is cutting down volatility in the market so the story isn't creating a lot of drama."  \par \par \uc2 It is widely believed that freeing the Chinese yuan from its peg of around 8.28 per dollar will result in an appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 China has come under heavy pressure from countries like the United States to allow the yuan to appreciate, as some manufacturers say an artificially cheap yuan hurts jobs and exports.  \par \par \uc2 Traders in New York paid little attention to mixed U.S. economic data released on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Orders for durable goods in October slipped, new home sales edged up, and the University of Michigan consumer confidence index for November showed a slightly higher reading than a month earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The first in a series of major data to be released on Thursday had little impact on the market.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's trade surplus rose 8.8 percent from a year earlier, better than forecasts for a rise of 2.8 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Traders were also awaiting current account data for September from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB), and the IFO index of the German business climate, due later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 The index is expected to show a fall to 94.7 in November from 95.3 the previous month.  \par \par \uc2 The chief economists of the ECB and the Bank of England are also expected to make speeches in separate appearances on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041125e0bp004mv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
