\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar edges up vs euro, but record lows in sight.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu  \par \uc2 438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2004\par 02:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 25 (Reuters) - The dollar inched up but stayed in sight of record lows against the euro on Thursday, as investors took a break from pummeling the dollar after showing little interest in a mixed bag of U.S. economic data.   \par \par \uc2  With U.S. markets closed for the Thanksgiving holiday, traders said they were waiting on economic data from Japan and Europe to decide whether to beat the dollar down past the record low of $1.3190 per euro hit just before the start of Tokyo trade.   \par \par \uc2  "It's hard to say if we'll be seeing any major moves today due to the holiday, but with some key data coming out, there will be plenty of opportunity for the dollar to fluctuate," said a strategist at a foreign bank.   \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, some analysts in Tokyo said that concerns about yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had calmed for the moment, given the apparent hesitation of the authorities to stray from usual rhetoric, despite the dollar's slide against the yen.   \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, former Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda repeated the government's stance on the importance of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability, and would not comment on the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Kuroda currently serves a special adviser to Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi.   \par \par \uc2  Some in the market say that such comments lack conviction, and they see the dollar's fall to 100 yen by the end of the calendar year as a real possibility.   \par \par \uc2  The last time the dollar was at that level was in November 1995.   \par \par \uc2  At 0125 GMT, the dollar was at around $1.3170 per euro compared with $1.3186 in late New York trade.   \par \par \uc2  It was at 102.85 yen, after grinding down to a four-and-a-half year low of 102.56 yen in overseas trading.   \par \par \uc2  Traders in New York paid little attention to mixed U.S. economic data.   \par \par \uc2  Orders for durable goods in October slipped, new home sales rose, and the University of Michigan consumer confidence index for November showed a slightly higher reading than a month earlier.   \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Labor Department said initial jobless claims dropped to a two-month low.   \par \par \uc2  The first in a series of major data to be released on Thursday had little impact on the market. Japan's trade surplus rose 8.8 percent from a year earlier, better than forecasts for a rise of 2.8 percent.   \par \par \uc2  Traders were also awaiting current account data for September from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the IFO index of the German business climate, due later in the day.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041125e0bp001bm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
