\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Govt Bonds End Lower As Dollar Holds Its Ground  \b0\par\par\uc2 897 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2004\par 07:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese government bond prices ended slightly lower Thursday as recovering stock prices and a failure of the yen to set fresh highs against the dollar in Asian trading encouraged investors to keep clear of the high-priced securities.  \par \par \uc2 JGBs rallied to fresh eight-month highs early after the dollar fell to a low of Y102.56 overnight, its weakest level in over four and a half years. But the dollar stayed in a narrow range during Asian trading, providing no new impetus to push up JGBs.  \par \par \uc2 Later in the session, dealers that bought new two-year JGBs at an auction Thursday found it difficult to place the high-priced securities and so sold some of the bonds, pushing the overall market lower.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think investors are comfortable to increase risks at these levels," said Masaaki Mizuno, chief fixed-income strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 December JGB futures ended 0.10 lower at 138.86, well below their intraday high of 139.18, which was the highest level for the contract since mid March. The yield on the newest 10-year JGBs was quoted one basis point higher in late trading at 1.410%, above its intraday low of 1.38%.  \par \par \uc2 "We think that 1.4% will continue to remain a strong resistance level," Mizuno said.  \par \par \uc2 Market participants have focused in recent days on the yen's rise, which puts a brake on the economy by making exports more expensive. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation also boosts deflationary pressures as imports become cheaper.  \par \par \uc2 In the short end of the curve, dealers struggled to sell new two-year bonds at high prices set early in the session.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctioned Y1.688 trillion of 0.1% two-year bonds with a lowest price of 100.02 to yield 0.089%. Foreign securities firms were the biggest buyers with UBS Securities purchasing Y300 billion, Morgan Stanley buying Y203.4 billion and JPMorgan Y200 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "In the secondary market, clients are sidelined so dealers that bought a lot are reducing positions," said a head trader from a foreign securities firm.  \par \par \uc2 JGBs were also encouraged lower by a recovery in stock prices with the Nikkei 225 Stock Average closing 0.26% higher at 10900.34.  \par \par \uc2 Prices At Around 0600 GMT:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Tiffe Jun 3-Mos Euroyen Price:    99.885  Dn 0.005 \par    SGX Jun 3-Mos Euroyen Price:      99.885  Dn 0.005 \par    TSE Dec 10-Yr JGB Future Price:   138.86  Dn 0.10 \par    SGX Dec 10-Yr JGB Future Price:   138.85  Dn 0.15 \par    10-Yr 1.5% JGB No. 264 Yield:     1.410%  Up 0.010 \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-25-04 0130ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants have said that JGB prices will continue to rise whenever the yen further appreciates against the dollar until \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decides it is time to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Policy makers in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have so far said only that they're concerned about the yen's rise and that now is the appropriate time to start considering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board member Hidehiko Haru told business leaders in Kumamoto, southern Japan, Thursday that a further and rapid yen rise against the dollar may hurt the economy by eroding the earnings of Japanese exporters.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We will closely watch" movements in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, he said. But he added that the problem isn't only Japan's. Current \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves are a matter of "overall dollar weakness" rather than a yen rise and are driven by concern over the U.S. current account and budget deficits, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some say any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would likely cap the rally in JGBs. But Dresdner's Mizuno said the impact would be only temporary.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In an initial reaction, (the JGB market) may sell off a little bit, but I don't think \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will have a sustainable impact," said Dresdner's Mizuno.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants generally said that aside from movements in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, investors are waiting for fresh news on the global and Japanese economies before taking fresh positions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mizuno said next week's U.S. nonfarm payrolls data will be a focus.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders also cited month-end buying by pension funds as a support for the market, but "flows are very thin," said the head trader.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After the market closed, MOF said that no primary dealers applied to buy additional new, two-year bonds in a non-price auction, in a further sign that the market has had its fill of the new securities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the interest-rate swap market, the curve ended slightly steeper toward the six- and seven-year zone. In afternoon trading, some rushed to unwind their receiving positions. But some Japanese banks and securities houses used the weaker market to rebuild their receiving positions. Market participants said many view today's weaker market as only temporary and expect renewed downward pressure on the yield curve toward the end of the year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The five-year swap rate ended 1.125 basis points higher at 0.68875% while the 10-year rate rose 0.875 basis point to 1.50625%. The seven-10-year spread was steady at 44.0 basis points.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Iain McDonald, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2941; iain.mcdonald@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Hiroshi Miwa contributed to this article).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Bennett Richardson [ 11-25-04 0216ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1037 4153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041125e0bp0000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
