\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slumps to new lows vs euro in thin trade.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2004\par 21:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 24 (Reuters) - The dollar dropped to fresh all-time lows against the euro on Wednesday in a pre-holiday-thinned session as a mixed batch of U.S. economic data did little to ease heavy selling pressure.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell to nine-year lows against the Swiss franc and 4-1/2 year troughs against the yen, weighed down by concerns over the massive U.S. trade deficit, the lack of action among U.S. policy-makers to slow the the dollar's slide and speculation some central banks are increasing their euro holdings.  \par \par \uc2 A somewhat mixed bag of U.S. data, including a fairly solid University of Michigan consumer sentiment number, also failed to boost the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Going into the U.S. Thanksgiving holiday, nothing significant has really occurred over the past week that could potentially reverse the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trend," said Kathy Lien, chief fundamental analyst at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Capital Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "With today's mixed data, we expect the euro to remain bid until Friday," she added.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading for the rest of the week is expected to thin as U.S. market participants celebrate Thursday's Thanksgiving Day holiday.  \par \par \uc2 Around mid-afternoon in New York, the euro was up at $1.3177 , just below the record high of $1.3178 reached earlier in the session, according to Reuters data.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the Swiss franc, the dollar slumped more than 1 percent to\par 1.1461 francs, near nine-year lows at 1.1450 Swiss francs  hit earlier\par in New York. Sterling, meanwhile, rose to $1.8804       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the yen, the dollar, which hit a new four-and-a-half year low around 102.58 yen earlier on Wednesday, was down at 102.87 yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LITTLE DOLLAR IMPACT FROM U.S. DATA  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier in the session, traders paid little attention to a slew of U.S. economic reports that showed mixed results.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The University of Michigan's final reading of its consumer confidence index for November was 92.8, up from a final October reading of 91.7. Wall Street analysts on average had forecast the index would rise to 96.0.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. orders for durable goods - big-ticket items like cars and refrigerators meant to last at least three years - slipped 0.4 percent in October. Excluding transport, orders fell 0.7 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The release of these numbers coincided with the dollar's latest leg down.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is not an environment where cyclical data matters all that much," said Bob Sinche, head of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Bank of America in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But this isn't about cyclical performance. It's about reserve reallocation and sluggish capital flows going into the year-end. And it seems like nobody wants to stand in the way at the moment."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given thinning trading conditions, traders were wary about the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger yen makes \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's exports more expensive and threatens the country's economic growth. The same applies to the euro and euro zone, but most analysts say Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which has a history of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , is more likely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and buy dollars than the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I'm sure there are a lot of traders out there who would be uneasy about leaving their positions on dollar/yen right at the lows," said Lara Rhame, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Jamie McGeever).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041124e0bo00d7c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
