\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar falls to record low vs euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu  \par \uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2004\par 08:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 24 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a record low against the euro on Wednesday, as news that Russia may consider boosting the share of euros in its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves gave traders another reason to dump the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said that a warning issued last week by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan about further dollar weakness also continued to resonate throughout the market.  \par \par \uc2 "You could say the dollar has hit rock bottom, for now, anyway, on the Greenspan comments," said Hideaki Furumaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Trust and Custody Services Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan warned that the U.S. deficits were unsustainable and that investor appetite for dollars was bound to dwindle.  \par \par \uc2 As of 0735 GMT, the dollar fetched around $1.3105 per euro It skidded as far as $1.3118, after spending most of Tokyo trade in tight ranges.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slumped to a nine-year low of 1.1560 Swiss francs.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling bought around $1.8705, not far from its four-month high of $1.8729 hit on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar met with fresh selling in New York on Tuesday when the first deputy chairman of the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Alexei Ulyukayev, said that the bank was reviewing its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves "with the highest priority".  \par \par \uc2 Speculation swirled in the market that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could further increase the share of euros in its $100 billion-plus reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Euros already account for 25-30 percent of the reserves, compared with 10 percent two years ago.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  NERVES  \par \par \uc2 A recent rebound in oil prices also helped to weaken the dollar, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices have crept up since late last week, reversing their decline since late October, with U.S. light crude futures briefly rising above $50 per barrel on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar firmed slightly to around 103.40 yen from 103.31 in New York, not far from a four-year low of 102.70 hit on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The yen showed limited reaction to slightly better than expected data on Japan's service industries.  \par \par \uc2 The tertiary sector index rose 0.1 percent in September, beating forecasts for a fall of 0.1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Traders were hesitant about buying the yen, due in part to worries that a further rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could prompt the Japanese authorities to restart dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry kept up its campaign of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, with a senior official chiding the market for allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves to fluctuate too rapidly and ignoring fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a senior official from Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party said the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be fleeting and minimal in light of the overall volume of trade. ($1=103.35 yen) (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano) (Editing by Brent Kininmont; Reuters messaging; naomi.tajitsu.reuters.com@reuters.net; Tel +81-3-3432-8657)).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041124e0bo006px\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clver